window.TOPO_DOC_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","href":"building/building-includes.html","text":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes ) building-includes is the mandatory project manifest. It selects the topo maps and transports that participate in one compiled building and records the ordered names of its map instances. It is a composition declaration, not textual inclusion. Source text is not pasted into another source, and a configuration cannot include another configuration."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Syntax","href":"building/building-includes.html#syntax","text":"building-includes { submap Lobby managed-by Public submap OfficeLevel28 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by Office vehicle MainElevator } The body accepts only submap and vehicle entries. Multiple entries must be separated by line breaks. Line and block comments are permitted. The supported forms are: submap MapName submap MapName managed-by DomainName submap InstanceName using BaseMapName submap InstanceName using BaseMapName managed-by DomainName vehicle TransportName When both clauses are present, using must precede managed-by . Each name must be a TopoScript identifier rather than a filename or path."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Project Sources","href":"building/building-includes.html#project-sources","text":"A project is composed from the following source categories:"},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"configuration.tcfg or configuration","href":"building/building-includes.html#project-sources","text":"The required building-includes source. When both names exist, configuration.tcfg takes precedence."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"root","href":"building/building-includes.html#project-sources","text":"An optional extensionless source containing project-global parameters, constraints, types, values, and functions. It is discovered automatically and is not listed in building-includes ."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Topo-map sources","href":"building/building-includes.html#project-sources","text":"Sources selected by submap entries, including base sources required by using ."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Transport sources","href":"building/building-includes.html#project-sources","text":"Sources selected by vehicle entries. Map and transport sources present in the project but not referenced by the manifest do not participate in compilation. A base map named by using participates even when it has no separate plain submap entry."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Source Resolution","href":"building/building-includes.html#source-resolution","text":"For a plain declaration submap Lobby , the directory compiler first searches for Lobby.tmap and then for the extensionless source Lobby . For vehicle MainElevator , it first searches for MainElevator.ttr and then for MainElevator . The in-memory compiler performs the same lookup using exact keys in the supplied filename-to-source map: configuration.tcfg root Lobby.tmap StandardOfficeFloor.tmap MainElevator.ttr Names in building-includes cannot contain directories, path separators, extensions, glob patterns, or URLs. All resolved sources therefore belong to one logical project-root namespace."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Direct Submaps","href":"building/building-includes.html#direct-submaps","text":"A plain submap entry selects a topo-map source with the same name: building-includes { submap Lobby } Lobby.tmap : topo-map Lobby() { let params = {} topo-node entrance } For predictable cross-file behavior, the manifest name, source filename, and top-level topo-map name must agree exactly, including case."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Reusable Submap Instances","href":"building/building-includes.html#reusable-submaps","text":"using constructs a named map instance from a reusable base topo map: building-includes { submap OfficeLevel28 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by Office } The compiler loads StandardOfficeFloor.tmap or StandardOfficeFloor . It does not search for OfficeLevel28.tmap . The compiled topology is exposed as an instance named OfficeLevel28 . Other sources refer to the instance name: station F28 at OfficeLevel28::elevator_lobby { location = 126.0, departureRate = 0.04 } using is shallow topology reuse. It does not import the base source into lexical scope, extend the base declaration, or recursively resolve another instance alias. The current compilation result may also retain the loaded base map as an internal graph entry. Applications must treat the declared instance names as the building maps and must not depend on the base template being exposed as a navigable map."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Management-Domain Attachment","href":"building/building-includes.html#management-domains","text":"Placeholder: management-domain attachment across direct maps and reusable map instances. managed-by associates a configured management domain with the resulting map or map instance: submap Lobby managed-by Public submap OfficeLevel28 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by Office The second declaration assigns Office to OfficeLevel28 ; it does not assign that domain to every instance of StandardOfficeFloor . Domain overrides and their routing effects are defined in the building-global declarations and routing-semantics articles."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Composition and Visibility","href":"building/building-includes.html#composition-and-visibility","text":"The configuration determines project membership, while the optional root supplies the global compilation environment. Root declarations are available while every selected topo map and transport is compiled. A configuration entry does not make declarations from one child source visible to another. Map-local declarations remain local to that map, and transport-local declarations remain local to that transport. Transports connect maps through qualified node names rather than lexical imports."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Compilation Order","href":"building/building-includes.html#compilation-order","text":"Placeholder: whole-building compilation from the project manifest to navigation and transport graphs. Parse configuration.tcfg or configuration . Load and elaborate the optional root . Load and elaborate directly declared topo maps. Load base topo maps required by using . Construct the named reusable map instances. Resolve effective management domains. Load and elaborate transports against the complete map collection. Construct the navigation graphs, transport graph, map sequence, and compilation metadata. A map or transport source may therefore use visible root declarations regardless of where its manifest entry appears. All selected and derived maps are available before transports are linked."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Source Order","href":"building/building-includes.html#source-order","text":"The order of submap entries is retained as the compiled graphSequence . Routing code may use this sequence, so authors must list map instances in the intended building order. The order of entries does not control file discovery: a reusable base or transport-referenced map need not be declared earlier than the entry that uses it. Vehicle order is retained internally by the current compiler but has no specified route-selection meaning."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Cross-File Name Resolution","href":"building/building-includes.html#cross-file-resolution","text":"A transport station resolves its qualified node reference in two stages: The map component must identify a compiled direct map or reusable instance. The node component must identify a node declared by that map’s topology. station Lobby at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.25 } Identifiers are case-sensitive. Lobby::main_elevator_landing , lobby::main_elevator_landing , and Lobby::Main_Elevator_Landing are distinct names. Every declared submap instance name must be unique across direct entries and using instances. Repeated vehicle declarations are also rejected. An instance created with using must not have the same name as any reusable base source selected by the configuration, because base maps are compiled under their own names. These checks run before map or transport sources are loaded. Distinct instances may share a base, and that base may also have one direct submap entry. The current compiler does not consistently diagnose a mismatch between a manifest name, its filename, and the top-level name declared inside that source. Such mismatches are invalid project authoring and must not be used for aliasing."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Missing References","href":"building/building-includes.html#missing-references","text":"Current behavior depends on the missing element: Condition Current result No configuration.tcfg or configuration Compilation fails. Invalid building-includes syntax Parsing fails with a syntax error. Missing directly declared topo-map source The compiler reports a warning and omits the map. Missing transport source The compiler reports a warning and omits the transport. Missing base source named by using The compiler reports a warning and cannot construct the corresponding instances. Transport references a map that was not constructed Transport linking fails. Transport references a node absent from its map Transport linking fails with a missing-node error. A successful compiler return does not currently prove that every manifest entry was loaded, because missing map and transport files are warnings rather than fatal diagnostics. Applications that require a complete building must treat these warnings as project validation failures."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Recursive and Cyclic Composition","href":"building/building-includes.html#recursive-and-cyclic-composition","text":"A building-includes source cannot include another configuration, so configuration recursion is not supported. using names a physical base topo-map source rather than another alias to resolve recursively. Alias chains, self-reference, and cycles such as the following are unsupported: building-includes { submap A using B submap B using A } Alias chains, self-reference, and cycles are rejected as collisions between instance names and reusable base names; the compiler does not recursively resolve aliases. Authors must use an independent base topo-map source for reusable instances."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Relationship to the Compiled Building","href":"building/building-includes.html#compiled-building-model","text":"The configuration contributes the direct map graphs, reusable map instances, ordered map sequence, configured management-domain information, and selected transports. The compiler links transport stations to nodes in those maps and produces one transport graph connecting the otherwise independent map graphs. The optional root contributes shared values used during compilation but does not create a navigable map. Files not selected by the manifest or by a using base reference do not contribute to the result."},{"page":"Whole-Building Composition ( building-includes )","title":"Portability and Packaging","href":"building/building-includes.html#portability-and-packaging","text":"A portable TopoScript project should: keep all compiler-visible sources in one project-root namespace; use exact, case-matching identifiers and filenames; prefer .tcfg , .tmap , and .ttr for their corresponding source categories; keep the optional root source extensionless; package every directly referenced source and every base source named by using ; avoid relying on filesystem case folding, traversal order, or undeclared files; store source text as UTF-8. The in-memory compiler receives source text directly. The directory compiler currently reads files using the runtime’s default character set rather than enforcing UTF-8, so non-ASCII source portability remains dependent on the host until decoding is standardized."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","href":"building/global-declarations.html","text":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs The optional extensionless root source establishes declarations shared by every topo map and transport in one TopoScript project. It does not create a map, node, path, transport, or navigable building area. Project membership remains the responsibility of building-includes . For the general relationship among buildings, maps, transports, parameters, and constraints, see Major Language Entities and Their Relationships ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"The Root Source","href":"building/global-declarations.html#root-source","text":"The root source is named root and contains one root declaration: root SkyrimTower( officeAccess: Bool, tradingFloorAccess: Bool, carryingWeight: Float, timeOfDayInSeconds: Int ) { // Building-global declarations } The identifier after root names the compiled root declaration. If a project needs neither declared compilation inputs nor shared declarations, the root source may be omitted. Source discovery and project layout are defined in Project Sources ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Documented Global Declarations","href":"building/global-declarations.html#declaration-inventory","text":"Declaration Purpose Root parameter Names an external value expected for compilation. def Defines a shared function or calculated value. constraint Gives one or more Boolean requirements a reusable name. Root-level let and type declarations are present in the current parser, but their intended long-term global role is under design review. They are not part of the stable building-global surface documented here. Although the shared body grammar can parse some topology or transport constructs inside a root, the root compiler does not elaborate them. Nodes, paths, stations, and ride-from declarations must be placed in their respective map or transport sources."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Compilation Inputs","href":"building/global-declarations.html#compilation-inputs","text":"Root parameters declare values that the containing application is expected to supply when compiling the project: root SkyrimTower( isInEmergency: Bool, officeAccess: Bool, carryingWeight: Float, timeOfDayInSeconds: Int ) { } The reliably supported external parameter types are Bool , Int , Float , and String . Their language meanings are defined in Primitive and Domain-Specific Types . The API compiler accepts host Boolean, integer, long-integer, single-precision floating-point, double-precision floating-point, and string values. Lists, records, functions, and arbitrary host objects are not currently accepted as external compilation inputs."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Required Values and Defaults","href":"building/global-declarations.html#required-values-and-defaults","text":"TopoScript does not currently provide parameter-default syntax. Default values are planned for a near-term language update. The following intended form is not yet valid: root Building( officeAccess: Bool = false ) { } Until defaults are implemented, an application should supply every declared parameter used by the project. The current compiler does not yet validate the root parameter list as a strict invocation contract: a missing parameter is detected only if evaluation reads it; an unused declared parameter may be omitted without an error; an undeclared extra parameter is accepted and becomes visible to child sources; a supplied value is not checked directly against the type written in the root parameter list. These behaviors are implementation limitations, not recommended authoring practices. Applications should supply exactly the declared parameters with values matching their declared types."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Global Functions","href":"building/global-declarations.html#global-functions","text":"A root-level def defines reusable calculation logic visible to maps and transports: root RunningExample() { def timeWhenRushing(stdCost: Float, timeToRun1KmInSeconds: Int): Float = { stdCost * (timeToRun1KmInSeconds / 300) // Assume 1km=5mins is the standard pace } } A map can use the function when calculating path data: topo-map Concourse() { let params = {} topo-node north_entrance topo-node platform_lobby atomic-path [north_entrance platform_lobby] { cost = timeWhenRushing(10.0, 240) } } This call produces a cost of 8.0 : integer division returns the fractional Float ratio 240 / 300 = 0.8 , which is multiplied by 10.0 . Function definitions are evaluated according to their dependency graph rather than their textual order. A cyclic dependency fails compilation. Parameter annotations participate in function construction, while declared return types are parsed but are not yet consistently enforced."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Global Constraints","href":"building/global-declarations.html#global-constraints","text":"A constraint gives a Boolean compilation requirement a reusable name: root SkyrimTower( officeAccess: Bool, carryingWeight: Float ) { constraint OfficeAccess { require officeAccess } constraint CarryingGoods { require carryingWeight > 0.0 } } Every require clause must evaluate to Bool . Multiple clauses form a conjunction: constraint AuthorizedOfficeDelivery { require officeAccess require carryingWeight > 0.0 } AuthorizedOfficeDelivery is satisfied only when every clause is true. An empty constraint is currently accepted and evaluates to true , but should be avoided because it communicates no policy. A complete applied example is available in SkyrimTower’s Operational Access Inputs ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Constraint Visibility","href":"building/global-declarations.html#constraint-visibility","text":"A root constraint is visible to every compiled topo map and transport. A child-local declaration may shadow a global name. The general scope rules are defined in Name Scopes and Visibility . A constraint has no effect merely because it exists. It affects the compiled building only when referenced by an access annotation or ride policy. atomic-path [public_hall staff_hall] { cost = 4.0 } requires OfficeAccess station F50 at OfficeLevel50::passenger_lift { location = 222.0, departureRate = 0.055 } requires TradingFloorAccess on Arrive ride-from any to OfficeDomain requires OfficeAccess"},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Constraint Evaluation Order","href":"building/global-declarations.html#constraint-evaluation-order","text":"Root definitions are evaluated in dependency order before root constraints. A root constraint may use supplied compilation parameters, root values, and root functions, regardless of where those definitions appear in the source. Map-local and transport-local constraints likewise run after their local definitions. root Building(weight: Int) { constraint CanEnter { require allowedWeight(weight) } def allowedWeight(w: Int): Bool = w Root constraints are then evaluated in source order. Root functions capture the root definition environment; they cannot depend on constraint results that have not yet been evaluated. Definition dependency cycles fail compilation. A constraint may refer to a previously evaluated constraint, but directly listing the required predicates is usually clearer. A reference to a later constraint is unresolved at the time of evaluation."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Effects of Hard Constraints","href":"building/global-declarations.html#hard-constraint-effects","text":"Attachment Effect when unsatisfied Atomic path The path is omitted from the compiled topology. Unscoped station requirement Both arrival and departure are prohibited. Station requirement on Arrive Arrival is prohibited; departure remains possible. Station requirement on Depart Departure is prohibited; arrival remains possible. ride-from requirement Matching directed transport rides are omitted. These are compile-time effects. Detailed attachment semantics belong to the topology, transport, and routing articles. A subject-to annotation is different: it records uncertain access rather than enforcing a hard prohibition. The tutorial examples compare the two models in Representing Uncertain Access ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Management Domains","href":"building/global-declarations.html#management-domains","text":"A management domain is a building-global map classification, but it is not declared as a root value. A domain name is introduced by assigning it to a submap: building-includes { submap Lobby managed-by PublicDomain submap OfficeLevel28 managed-by OfficeDomain } A topo map may replace its configured domain: topo-map OfficeLevel28() override-managed-by TradingDomain { let params = {} topo-node elevator_lobby } The effective domain is the topo-map override when present, otherwise the configuration assignment, otherwise the fallback domain Misc . Each compiled map has one effective domain. Domain attachment is introduced in Management-Domain Attachment . A realistic multi-domain example is provided by Management Domains and Ride Policies ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Access and Ride-Policy Model","href":"building/global-declarations.html#access-policy-model","text":"TopoScript has no standalone global access-policy declaration. The model combines: a global or local named constraint; a requires attachment on a path or station; or a ride-from declaration inside a transport. root Building(hasHotelAccess: Bool) { constraint HotelAccess { require hasHotelAccess } } ride-from any to HotelDomain requires HotelAccess The root defines the reusable condition. The map or transport defines where that condition applies."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Building Metadata and Global Tags","href":"building/global-declarations.html#building-metadata-and-tags","text":"TopoScript currently has no dedicated building-metadata declaration for an address, operator, display name, geographic location, publication version, or similar project-level information. Such information must be maintained by the containing application or project package. There is likewise no global tag declaration or tag registry. Tags appearing in node or path records are application-facing annotation values rather than globally declared language entities. See Stability Boundaries ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Global Names","href":"building/global-declarations.html#global-names","text":"Parameter, function, and constraint names belong to the root’s project-global scope. Authors should keep each name unique and avoid collisions between declaration categories. The current compiler may silently replace colliding global bindings. Name collision must not be used as a policy mechanism. Child-local shadowing is supported by the general lexical-scope model, but should be deliberate and clearly documented."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Compilation Time and Routing Time","href":"building/global-declarations.html#compilation-versus-routing","text":"Root parameters and constraints are evaluated while compiling the building: TopoScript sources + compilation-input values | v fixed compiled graph and access state | v one or more route requests A route request does not independently re-evaluate root constraints. Changing officeAccess , timeOfDayInSeconds , or another compilation input requires a separately compiled result for that input context. Routing preferences and banned tags belong to the route request rather than the root. The full lifecycle is defined in Language Lifecycle ."},{"page":"Building-Global Declarations and Compilation Inputs","title":"Effects on the Compiled Building","href":"building/global-declarations.html#compiled-effects","text":"Global feature Possible effect Parameter Supplies data used by expressions and constraints. def Supplies reusable calculation logic used during map or transport construction. Constraint attached to a path Changes graph connectivity. Constraint attached to a station Changes arrival and departure permission. Constraint attached to ride-from Changes available directed transport rides. Management domain Changes which maps match a domain-based ride policy. Unused declaration Has no direct effect. Global declarations are not returned as navigable route steps. Their observable result is the building model produced from them."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html","text":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials Status: Draft"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Purpose","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#tutorial-purpose","text":"This tutorial series introduces TopoScript one feature at a time. Each tutorial uses a fictional but realistic indoor venue, presents valid source code, and contrasts it with code that is invalid or misleading. Examples are classified as follows:"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Compilation-invalid","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#tutorial-purpose","text":"The TopoNavi Engine rejects the source or supplied compilation parameters."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Modeling-invalid","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#tutorial-purpose","text":"The source may compile, but it does not truthfully represent the physical venue."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Unsupported assumption","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#tutorial-purpose","text":"The source compiles, but the author expects behavior that the TopoNavi Engine does not provide. All venues and operational details in these tutorials are synthetic. File extensions are shown for clarity; the compiler also supports the documented extensionless source names."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Contents","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#tutorial-purpose","text":"Creating a Minimal Building Project Modeling Maps and Nodes Connecting Nodes with Atomic Paths Directed and Bidirectional Movement Adding Custom Node and Path Annotations Using Values and Expressions Parameters and Access Constraints Reusing a Standard Floor Map Connecting Floors with an Elevator Modeling Stairs and Escalators Management Domains and Ride Policies Representing Uncertain Access"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"1. Creating a Minimal Building Project","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#minimal-building","text":"Scenario: A neighborhood library has one public floor, one street entrance, and one reception area. Real-place analogy: The building configuration resembles the directory at the entrance: it identifies the mapped areas that belong to this venue. The topo-map then describes movement within one of those areas."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#minimal-building","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap LibraryGroundFloor } LibraryGroundFloor.tmap topo-map LibraryGroundFloor() { let params = { permResident = 0 } topo-node street_entrance topo-node reception atomic-path [street_entrance reception] { cost = 12.0 } } The project compiles one map containing two nodes and one path in each direction. A route can be requested between LibraryGroundFloor::street_entrance and LibraryGroundFloor::reception ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: referenced source is absent","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#minimal-building","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap LibraryGroundFloor } If neither LibraryGroundFloor.tmap nor an extensionless LibraryGroundFloor source is supplied, the project is incomplete. The current compiler reports a missing-map warning and cannot produce the intended library graph. Related reference: Source-File and Project Boundaries"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"2. Modeling Maps and Nodes","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#maps-and-nodes","text":"Scenario: A clinic floor contains a reception area, a waiting area, a corridor corner, and the area immediately outside an examination room. Real-place analogy: Each node is a bounded area where a visitor can arrive, depart, enter another space, or make a navigation decision. It is not a pin placed somewhere inside a larger overlapping node."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#maps-and-nodes","text":"ClinicFloor.tmap topo-map ClinicFloor() { let params = { permResident = 18 } topo-node reception_area topo-node waiting_area topo-node corridor_corner_NE topo-node exam_room_3_outside atomic-path [reception_area waiting_area] { cost = 6.0 } atomic-path [waiting_area corridor_corner_NE] { cost = 8.0 } atomic-path [corridor_corner_NE exam_room_3_outside] { cost = 5.0 } } Each name denotes a distinct, non-overlapping physical area. Neighboring areas may touch at their boundaries, but their interiors do not intersect."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: overlapping node meanings","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#maps-and-nodes","text":"ClinicFloor.tmap topo-map ClinicFloor() { let params = { permResident = 18 } // Intended to represent the entire corridor, including its northeast corner. topo-node entire_corridor // Intended to represent an area contained inside entire_corridor. topo-node corridor_corner_NE atomic-path [entire_corridor corridor_corner_NE] { cost = 1.0 } } Classification: Modeling-invalid. The compiler has no venue geometry with which to detect the overlap. The author must partition the corridor into non-overlapping node areas instead. Related reference: Node"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"3. Connecting Nodes with Atomic Paths","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#atomic-paths","text":"Scenario: A museum corridor leads from the public entrance past an information desk to an exhibition entrance. Real-place analogy: A visitor walking to the exhibition necessarily crosses the information-desk area. The corridor must therefore be split at that navigation-relevant area."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#atomic-paths","text":"topo-node public_entrance topo-node information_desk topo-node exhibition_entrance atomic-path [public_entrance information_desk] { cost = 9.0 } atomic-path [information_desk exhibition_entrance] { cost = 14.0 } The route from the entrance to the exhibition passes through information_desk ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: skipping an intermediate node","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#atomic-paths","text":"atomic-path [public_entrance exhibition_entrance] { cost = 23.0 } Classification: Modeling-invalid if this physical passage necessarily crosses the information-desk node. The direct path is valid only if it represents a genuinely separate traversal that bypasses the desk."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: missing path cost","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#atomic-paths","text":"atomic-path [information_desk exhibition_entrance] { description = \"Main exhibition corridor\" } Classification: Compilation-invalid. Every atomic path must contain a numeric cost . Related reference: Atomic Path"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"4. Directed and Bidirectional Movement","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#path-direction","text":"Scenario: A supermarket has a one-way entrance gate, a separate exit gate, and ordinary two-way aisles. Real-place analogy: Direction describes how people may actually move. The entrance gate admits customers but does not provide the return journey, while an aisle permits travel both ways."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#path-direction","text":"topo-node street_entrance topo-node entrance_lobby topo-node aisle_junction topo-node checkout_hall atomic-path [street_entrance -> entrance_lobby] { cost = 4.0 } atomic-path [entrance_lobby aisle_junction] { cost = 10.0 } atomic-path [aisle_junction checkout_hall] { cost = 12.0 } The first path permits entry only. Each bidirectional declaration produces traversal in both directions."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: false reverse access","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#path-direction","text":"// Incorrect if the physical gate permits entry only. atomic-path [street_entrance entrance_lobby] { cost = 4.0 } Classification: Modeling-invalid. This declaration creates a return traversal that the venue does not permit. Related reference: Atomic Path"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"5. Adding Custom Node and Path Annotations","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#custom-annotations","text":"Scenario: A shopping center application displays tenant descriptions and distinguishes indoor and covered outdoor passages. Real-place analogy: An annotation resembles information attached to an entry in a venue directory. TopoScript can carry that information without deciding how every application must display or interpret it."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#custom-annotations","text":"topo-node bakery_entrance { description = \"Entrance beside the central atrium\", tenantCategory = \"food_and_drink\", directoryAliases = [\"Bakery\", \"Cafe\"] } topo-node atrium_walkway atomic-path [atrium_walkway bakery_entrance] { cost = 7.0, passageType = \"indoor\" } The custom values are preserved as structured node and path attributes. A consuming application may expose them as JSON-compatible strings and lists."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: expecting automatic business-hours enforcement","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#custom-annotations","text":"topo-node pharmacy_entrance { businessHours = \"09:00-18:00\", openNow = false } topo-node mall_corridor atomic-path [mall_corridor pharmacy_entrance] { cost = 5.0 } Classification: Unsupported assumption. These custom annotations do not automatically remove the pharmacy path from the compiled topology. Business-hours behavior must be implemented by the application or expressed through supported compilation parameters and constraints. Related reference: Stability Boundaries"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"6. Using Values and Expressions","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#values-and-expressions","text":"Scenario: Several airport-concourse paths include the same expected delay for passing through a fire door. Real-place analogy: A shared value works like using one agreed door-delay estimate throughout a facilities plan instead of manually repeating the calculation."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#values-and-expressions","text":"let fireDoorDelay = 2.0 let shortCorridorTime = 8.0 topo-node gate_lounge topo-node fire_door_landing topo-node transfer_corridor atomic-path [gate_lounge fire_door_landing] { cost = shortCorridorTime + fireDoorDelay } atomic-path [fire_door_landing transfer_corridor] { cost = 12.0 + fireDoorDelay } The expressions are evaluated during compilation, producing numeric costs of 10.0 and 14.0 ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: mixing numeric types","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#values-and-expressions","text":"let corridorSeconds = 8 atomic-path [gate_lounge fire_door_landing] { cost = corridorSeconds + 2.0 } Classification: Compilation-invalid. Addition, subtraction, and multiplication do not implicitly convert between Int and Float ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example: fractional division","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#values-and-expressions","text":"let halfTransferTime = 30.0 / 2.0 Classification: Valid. The result is 15.0 . Division accepts any pair of integer or floating-point operands and always returns a Float ; 3 / 2 therefore produces 1.5 . A zero denominator or a nonfinite operand or result fails with an arithmetic error."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: unknown value","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#values-and-expressions","text":"atomic-path [gate_lounge fire_door_landing] { cost = measuredDoorDelay + 8.0 } Classification: Compilation-invalid because measuredDoorDelay is not defined in a visible scope. Related reference: Literals, Expressions, Operators, and Evaluation"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"7. Parameters and Access Constraints","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#parameters-and-constraints","text":"Scenario: An office has a public reception route and a shorter staff-only entrance to its records room. Real-place analogy: The physical corridor is unchanged, but the compiled accessible topology depends on whether the intended user has staff access."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#parameters-and-constraints","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap OfficeFloor } root root OfficeBuilding(hasStaffAccess: Bool) { constraint StaffOnly { require hasStaffAccess } } OfficeFloor.tmap topo-map OfficeFloor() { let params = { permResident = 45 } topo-node reception topo-node public_corridor topo-node staff_door topo-node records_room atomic-path [reception public_corridor] { cost = 8.0 } atomic-path [public_corridor records_room] { cost = 20.0 } atomic-path [reception staff_door] { cost = 5.0 } atomic-path [staff_door records_room] { cost = 4.0 } requires StaffOnly } Compiling with the following parameter makes the staff path available: { \"userParams\": { \"hasStaffAccess\": true } } Compiling with false excludes the constrained path. Changing the parameter requires another compilation."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: required parameter omitted","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#parameters-and-constraints","text":"{ \"userParams\": {} } Classification: Compilation-invalid because hasStaffAccess has no supplied value."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: parameter has the wrong type","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#parameters-and-constraints","text":"{ \"userParams\": { \"hasStaffAccess\": \"yes\" } } Classification: Compilation-invalid. The constraint requires a Boolean predicate, but this value evaluates as a String . Related reference: Parameters and Constraints"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"8. Reusing a Standard Floor Map","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#reusable-maps","text":"Scenario: Hotel guest floors 8, 9, and 10 have the same corridor and room-layout topology. Real-place analogy: The floors were built from the same architectural plan, but each remains a separately named place that an elevator can serve."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#reusable-maps","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap Floor8 using StandardGuestFloor submap Floor9 using StandardGuestFloor submap Floor10 using StandardGuestFloor } StandardGuestFloor.tmap topo-map StandardGuestFloor() { let params = { permResident = 72 } topo-node elevator_lobby topo-node corridor_junction topo-node room_01_outside atomic-path [elevator_lobby corridor_junction] { cost = 9.0 } atomic-path [corridor_junction room_01_outside] { cost = 11.0 } } The compiled building contains distinct maps named Floor8 , Floor9 , and Floor10 , each reusing the standard topology."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: unknown base map","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#reusable-maps","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap Floor8 using StandardGuestFlorr } Classification: Invalid project. If no source named StandardGuestFlorr.tmap or StandardGuestFlorr exists, the compiler reports that the base map is missing and cannot construct the intended floor instance. Related reference: Map"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"9. Connecting Floors with an Elevator","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#elevators","text":"Scenario: A three-level office building has one two-car elevator bank serving the lobby and two office floors. Real-place analogy: A map node represents the landing area. An elevator station represents this elevator bank's use of that landing."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#elevators","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap Lobby submap Office2 submap Office3 vehicle MainElevator } Each referenced map contains its own landing node. Lobby.tmap topo-map Lobby() { let params = { permResident = 6 } topo-node main_elevator_landing } Office2.tmap topo-map Office2() { let params = { permResident = 80 } topo-node main_elevator_landing } Office3.tmap topo-map Office3() { let params = { permResident = 75 } topo-node main_elevator_landing } MainElevator.ttr transport MainElevator is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 1000, capacity = 13, carAmount = 2 } let displayName = \"Main elevator bank\" station Lobby at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station Floor2 at Office2::main_elevator_landing { location = 4.2, departureRate = 0.10 } station Floor3 at Office3::main_elevator_landing { location = 8.4, departureRate = 0.10 } } The stations connect three qualified map nodes through one elevator. The transport parameters are used by the TopoNavi Engine when estimating elevator travel."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: incomplete station data","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#elevators","text":"station Floor2 at Office2::main_elevator_landing { location = 4.2 } Classification: Compilation-invalid. An elevator station requires a floating-point departureRate ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: unknown landing node","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#elevators","text":"station Floor3 at Office3::service_elevator_landing { location = 8.4, departureRate = 0.10 } Classification: Compilation-invalid if service_elevator_landing is not declared by Office3 ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: no possible inter-map ride","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#elevators","text":"transport MainElevator is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.0 } station Lobby at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.10 } } Classification: Modeling-invalid. One station cannot provide travel between maps; an operational elevator model requires at least two station maps. Related reference: Transport and Station"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"10. Modeling Stairs and Escalators","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"Scenario: A railway concourse has a staircase between three levels and an escalator between the lower concourse and platform level. Real-place analogy: Stair stations identify landings along a continuous stair run. An escalator is modeled as one timed connection between exactly two station maps. configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap StreetLevel submap LowerConcourse submap PlatformLevel vehicle ConcourseStairs vehicle PlatformEscalator } The three topo maps declare the corresponding landing nodes. StreetLevel.tmap topo-map StreetLevel() { let params = { permResident = 0 } topo-node stair_landing topo-node escalator_landing } LowerConcourse.tmap topo-map LowerConcourse() { let params = { permResident = 30 } topo-node stair_landing topo-node escalator_landing } PlatformLevel.tmap topo-map PlatformLevel() { let params = { permResident = 15 } topo-node stair_landing topo-node escalator_landing }"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid stairs example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"ConcourseStairs.ttr transport ConcourseStairs is Stairs { let params = { turnBackCost = 3 } station Street at StreetLevel::stair_landing { location = 0.0, directSegmentIndex = 0 } station Concourse at LowerConcourse::stair_landing { location = -4.0, directSegmentIndex = 1 } station Platform at PlatformLevel::stair_landing { location = -8.0, directSegmentIndex = 2 } }"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid stairs example: missing segment index","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"station Platform at PlatformLevel::stair_landing { location = -8.0 } Classification: Compilation-invalid. A stair station requires an integer directSegmentIndex ."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid escalator example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"PlatformEscalator.ttr transport PlatformEscalator is Escalator { let params = { time = 18.0 } let displayName = \"Platform escalator\" station Concourse at LowerConcourse::escalator_landing {} station Platform at PlatformLevel::escalator_landing {} } The escalator has exactly two stations and a positive numeric traversal time."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid escalator example: missing traversal time","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"transport PlatformEscalator is Escalator { let params = {} station Concourse at LowerConcourse::escalator_landing {} station Platform at PlatformLevel::escalator_landing {} } Classification: Compilation-invalid because params.time is required."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid escalator example: three stations","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#stairs-and-escalators","text":"transport PlatformEscalator is Escalator { let params = { time = 18.0 } station Street at StreetLevel::escalator_landing {} station Concourse at LowerConcourse::escalator_landing {} station Platform at PlatformLevel::escalator_landing {} } Classification: Compilation-invalid. An escalator currently connects exactly two distinct maps. Related reference: Transport"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"11. Management Domains and Ride Policies","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#management-domains","text":"Scenario: A mixed-use tower contains three public floors, three office floors, and three hotel floors. One elevator physically serves all nine floors. Real-place analogy: A management domain resembles the operational boundary between the retail podium, office landlord, and hotel operator. Each domain contains several separately named floors. A ride policy can describe movement between whole groups of floors without listing every floor pair."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#management-domains","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap GroundLobby using StandardPublicFloor managed-by PublicDomain submap RetailLevel1 using StandardPublicFloor managed-by PublicDomain submap RetailLevel2 using StandardPublicFloor managed-by PublicDomain submap OfficeLevel10 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by OfficeDomain submap OfficeLevel11 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by OfficeDomain submap OfficeLevel12 using StandardOfficeFloor managed-by OfficeDomain submap HotelLevel20 using StandardHotelFloor managed-by HotelDomain submap HotelLevel21 using StandardHotelFloor managed-by HotelDomain submap HotelLevel22 using StandardHotelFloor managed-by HotelDomain vehicle MixedUseLift } GroundLobby , OfficeLevel11 , and HotelLevel22 are map identifiers. PublicDomain , OfficeDomain , and HotelDomain are management-domain identifiers shared by three maps each. root root MixedUseTower(hasHotelAccess: Bool) { constraint HotelAccess { require hasHotelAccess } } The three reusable topo maps describe the standard topology for the floors in their respective domains. StandardPublicFloor.tmap topo-map StandardPublicFloor() { let params = { permResident = 30 } topo-node lift_landing topo-node public_corridor atomic-path [lift_landing public_corridor] { cost = 8.0 } } StandardOfficeFloor.tmap topo-map StandardOfficeFloor() { let params = { permResident = 120 } topo-node lift_landing topo-node office_corridor atomic-path [lift_landing office_corridor] { cost = 10.0 } } StandardHotelFloor.tmap topo-map StandardHotelFloor() { let params = { permResident = 90 } topo-node lift_landing topo-node guest_corridor atomic-path [lift_landing guest_corridor] { cost = 12.0 } } MixedUseLift.ttr transport MixedUseLift is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.5, acceleration = 0.8, carAmount = 2 } station LobbyStation at GroundLobby::lift_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station Retail1Station at RetailLevel1::lift_landing { location = 4.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station Retail2Station at RetailLevel2::lift_landing { location = 8.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station O10Station at OfficeLevel10::lift_landing { location = 40.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station O11Station at OfficeLevel11::lift_landing { location = 44.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station O12Station at OfficeLevel12::lift_landing { location = 48.0, departureRate = 0.10 } station H20Station at HotelLevel20::lift_landing "},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: using a map name as a ride-policy operand","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#management-domains","text":"// OfficeLevel11 is a map name, not a domain or station label. ride-from OfficeLevel11 to HotelDomain requires HotelAccess Classification: Compilation-invalid because OfficeLevel11 is neither a management-domain identifier nor a station label in this transport. To cover all three office floors, use OfficeDomain . To cover only the eleventh-floor station, use O11Station . Ride-policy operands are management domains, station labels, or any . Related reference: Management Domain"},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"12. Representing Uncertain Access","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#uncertain-access","text":"Scenario: A university skybridge has a card reader, but the map publisher cannot guarantee whether a particular visitor's card will be accepted. Real-place analogy: The bridge physically exists and might be usable. It should not be represented as certainly open or certainly inaccessible when the available information supports neither claim."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Valid example","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#uncertain-access","text":"configuration.tcfg building-includes { submap AcademicBuilding } root root CampusNavigation(hasBridgeCard: Bool) { constraint BridgeCardAccess { require hasBridgeCard } } AcademicBuilding.tmap topo-map AcademicBuilding() { let params = { permResident = 350 } topo-node atrium topo-node indoor_detour topo-node skybridge_door topo-node library_link atomic-path [atrium indoor_detour] { cost = 20.0 } atomic-path [indoor_detour library_link] { cost = 20.0 } atomic-path [atrium skybridge_door] { cost = 8.0 } atomic-path [skybridge_door library_link] { cost = 8.0 } subject-to BridgeCardAccess because \"The skybridge door may require a compatible university card.\" } The uncertain path remains represented with its failed or satisfied access condition. The TopoNavi Engine can prefer the confirmed detour and disclose the skybridge uncertainty when an uncertain route is considered."},{"page":"Feature-by-Feature Tutorials","title":"Invalid example: combining hard and uncertain access","href":"examples/feature-by-feature-tutorials.html#uncertain-access","text":"atomic-path [skybridge_door library_link] { cost = 8.0 } requires BridgeCardAccess subject-to BridgeCardAccess because \"The door may require a compatible university card.\" Classification: Compilation-invalid. The same element cannot use both requires and subject-to . Use requires for hard exclusion or subject-to for explicitly uncertain availability. Related reference: Parameters and Constraints The complete SkyrimTower example is documented separately. It combines the stable features introduced here into one coherent mock building."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html","text":"SkyrimTower Mock Building Status: Draft — transport specification; topo-map sources deferred"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Scope","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#scope","text":"SkyrimTower is a synthetic mixed-use supertall building used to demonstrate stable TopoScript features in a realistic, integrated project. This article currently defines the building program, map-naming assumptions, compilation-time access inputs, station-level controls, and the complete elevator inventory. Topo-map bodies are intentionally excluded from this draft. Every qualified station reference therefore establishes a node that the corresponding topo map must declare later."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Building Program","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":""},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels 72–75","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Observatory"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 71","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Mechanical or refuge level"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels 52–70","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Hotel, with the hotel lobby on Level 55"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 51","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Mechanical, refuge, and hotel-service transfer level"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels 31–50","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Upper office floors"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 30","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Office sky lobby"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 29","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Mechanical or refuge level"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels 8–28","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Lower office floors"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 7","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Separate mall-only upper level"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels 2–6","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Mall, with the principal tower connection on Level 5"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level 1","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Main lobby and lower mall connection"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Level B1","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Logistics"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Levels B2–B3","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-program","text":"Carpark The highest modeled landing is approximately 329.9 metres above the Level 1 datum. Coordinates remain unnecessary; station location values supply the vertical positions required by elevator calculations."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Map-Identifier Contract","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#map-identifiers","text":"The later topo-map sources must use the identifiers referenced by the transport files. Important special cases are: MainLobby represents the connected tower-and-mall area on Level 1. SharedLevel5 represents the connected tower-and-mall area on Level 5. MallLevel7 is separate from the main tower and is served only through mall circulation. RefugeLevel29 , ServiceRefugeLevel51 , and RefugeLevel71 represent the omitted mechanical or refuge floors. Every station reference of the form MapName::landing_name requires a matching node in MapName . The future submap declarations must assign these management domains: CarparkDomain : Levels B3 and B2. LogisticsDomain : Level B1. PublicDomain : the main lobby and shared Level 5. MallDomain : Mall Levels 2–4 and 6–7. Shared Level 5 remains in PublicDomain because it is a single connected tower–mall map. OfficeDomain : occupied office floors and the Level 30 sky lobby. RefugeDomain : Levels 29, 51, and 71. HotelDomain : Levels 52–70. ObservatoryDomain : Levels 72–75."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Operational Access Inputs","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#root-access-policy","text":"The elevator systems use destination-sensitive ride policies and station-specific access controls based on emergency state, destination access, carried goods, mall operation hours, and trading-floor authorization. Physical operator control remains part of the fire-service and office-service elevator descriptions, but is not represented as a routing parameter. root root SkyrimTower( isInEmergency: Bool, officeAccess: Bool, tradingFloorAccess: Bool, hotelAccess: Bool, observatoryAccess: Bool, logisticsAccess: Bool, carryingWeight: Float, timeOfDayInSeconds: Int ) { constraint InEmergency { require isInEmergency } constraint OfficeAccess { require officeAccess } constraint TradingFloorAccess { require tradingFloorAccess } constraint HotelAccess { require hotelAccess } constraint ObservatoryAccess { require observatoryAccess } constraint LogisticsAccess { require logisticsAccess } constraint CarryingGoods { require carryingWeight > 0.0 } constraint InMallOperationHours { require timeOfDayInSeconds > 35999 require timeOfDayInSeconds CarryingGoods converts the numeric rule carryingWeight > 0.0 into a named constraint usable by ride-from . timeOfDayInSeconds is an integer number of seconds since midnight. Because the current language supports strict comparisons, the bounds above admit every integer from 36000 (10:00:00) through 82800 (23:00:00), inclusive. The hotel service elevators intentionally have no operator or card-reader policy."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Building Composition Fragment","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#building-composition","text":"The vehicle declarations below are ready for the eventual configuration.tcfg . Submap declarations are deliberately deferred, so this fragment is not yet a complete compilable building project. building-includes { // Submap declarations will be added with the floor topology. vehicle OfficeZone1 vehicle OfficeZone2 vehicle OfficeExpress vehicle OfficeZone3 vehicle OfficeZone4 vehicle HotelExpress vehicle HotelLocal vehicle ObservatoryExpress vehicle ObservatoryLocal vehicle MainBuildingCarpark vehicle MallMain vehicle MallFreight vehicle FireService vehicle OfficeServ vehicle HotelServExpress vehicle HotelServLocal }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Elevator Summary","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#elevator-summary","text":"System Stops Cars Maximum velocity (m/s) Ride access OfficeZone1 F1, F5, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, F13, F14, F15 4 4.0 No ride policy OfficeZone2 F1, F5, F15, F16, F17, F18, F19, F20, F21, F22, F23, F24, F25, F26, F27, F28 5 5.0 F18 locked; F28 requires trading-floor access on arrival OfficeExpress F1, F5, F30 4 7.0 No ride policy OfficeZone3 F30, F31, F32, F33, F34, F35, F36, F37, F38, F39, F40 4 4.0 No ride policy OfficeZone4 F30, F40, F41, F42, F43, F44, F45, F46, F47, F48, F49, F50 4 5.0 F50 requires trading-floor access on arrival HotelExpress F1, F55 4 10.0 No ride policy HotelLocal F52, F53, F54, F55, F56, F57, F58, F59, F60, F61, F62, F63, F64, F65, F66, F67, F68, F69, F70 6 5.0 No ride policy ObservatoryExpress F5, F72, F73 4 12.0 No ride policy ObservatoryLocal F72, F73, F74, F75 1 3.0 No ride policy MainBuildingCarpark B3, B2, F1, F5 3 3.0 No ride policy MallMain B3, B2, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7 4 2.5 Mall operation-hours policy MallFreight B3, B2, B1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6 1 0.8 Mall operation-hours policy FireService B3, B2, B1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, F13, F14, F15, F16, F17, F18, F19, F20, F21, F22, F23, F24, F25, F26, F27, F28, F29, F30, F31, F32, F33, F34, F35, F36, F37, F38, F39, F40, F41, F42, F43, F44, F45, F46, F47, F48, F49, F50, F51, F52, F53, F54, F55, F56, F57, F58, F59, F60, F61, F62, F63, F64, F65, F66, F67, F68, F69, F70, F71, F72, F73, F74, F75 2 6.0 Refuge-emergency, mall-hours, and destination-access policies OfficeServ B1, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, F13, F14, F15, F16, F17, F18, F19, F20, F21, F22, F23, F24, F25, F26, F27, F28, F29, F30, F31, F32, F33, F34, F35, F36, F37, F38, F39, F40, F41, F42, F43, F44, F45, F46, F47, F48, F49, F50 2 3.0 Carried-goods and destination-domain policies HotelServExpress B2, B1, F51, F52, F53, F54, F55, F56, F57, F58, F59, F60, F61, F62, F63, F64, F65, F66, F67, F68, F69, F70 2 4.0 No ride policy HotelServLocal F51, F52, F53, F54, F55, F56, F57, F58, F59, F60, F61, F62, F63, F64, F65, F66, F67, F68, F69, F70 1 2.5 No ride policy"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Transport Source Conventions","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#transport-source-conventions","text":"All elevator stations use globally qualified node identifiers and explicit vertical locations. Each departureRate distribution is normalized approximately to one within its elevator system. Transfer floors receive greater rates than ordinary destination floors. The unannotated car counts and motion parameters are realistic modeling choices for this synthetic example, not universal engineering requirements. They may be refined when representative route outputs are calibrated."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"Ride-Policy Model","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#ride-policy-model","text":"A rule such as ride-from any to OfficeDomain applies to every origin station on the transport and every destination station whose map belongs to OfficeDomain . The rule does not use a floor identifier as its operand. The mall elevators require InMallOperationHours for destinations in MallDomain . Their shared Level 5 station is covered by a separate station-specific rule because its map belongs to PublicDomain . The fire-service elevator does not generally require an active emergency. Carpark and public destinations are unrestricted, mall destinations require normal mall operation hours, and controlled operational domains require their respective access permissions. Only destinations in RefugeDomain require InEmergency . The office-service elevator distinguishes office, logistics, and refuge destinations. Every ride requires CarryingGoods ; office and logistics destinations additionally require access to their respective domains. The passenger stations on trading Floors 28 and 50 require TradingFloorAccess only on arrival; an unauthorized user may still depart from either floor. The passenger station on Office Level 18 is out-of-order , which prohibits both arrival and departure through that station."},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"1. OfficeZone1","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-zone1","text":"Serves the lobby, the Level 5 tower–mall connection, and Office Levels 8–15. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 4.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 21. OfficeZone1.ttr transport OfficeZone1 is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 4.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 1600, capacity = 21, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Office Zone 1 elevators\" station F1 at MainLobby::office_zone_1_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.35000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::office_zone_1_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.15000} station F8 at OfficeLevel8::office_zone_1_landing {location = 42.0, departureRate = 0.06250} station F9 at OfficeLevel9::office_zone_1_landing {location = 46.2, departureRate = 0.06250} station F10 at OfficeLevel10::office_zone_1_landing {location = 50.4, departureRate = 0.06250} station F11 at OfficeLevel11::office_zone_1_landing {location = 54.6, departureRate = 0.06250} station F12 at OfficeLevel12::office_zone_1_landing {location = 58.8, departureRate = 0.06250} station F13 at OfficeLevel13::office_zone_1_landing {location = 63.0, departureRate = 0.06250} station F14 at OfficeLevel14::office_zone_1_landing {location = 67.2, departureRate = 0.06250} station F15 at OfficeLevel15::office_zone_1_landing {location = 71.4, departureRate = 0.06250} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"2. OfficeZone2","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-zone2","text":"Serves the lobby, Level 5, and Office Levels 15–28. Level 15 provides overlap with OfficeZone1. The selected parameters use 5 cars, a maximum velocity of 5.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 21. OfficeZone2.ttr transport OfficeZone2 is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 5.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 1600, capacity = 21, carAmount = 5 } let displayName = \"Office Zone 2 elevators\" station F1 at MainLobby::office_zone_2_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.30000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::office_zone_2_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.14000} station F15 at OfficeLevel15::office_zone_2_landing {location = 71.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F16 at OfficeLevel16::office_zone_2_landing {location = 75.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F17 at OfficeLevel17::office_zone_2_landing {location = 79.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F18 at OfficeLevel18::office_zone_2_landing {location = 84.0, departureRate = 0.04000} out-of-order station F19 at OfficeLevel19::office_zone_2_landing {location = 88.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F20 at OfficeLevel20::office_zone_2_landing {location = 92.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F21 at OfficeLevel21::office_zone_2_landing {location = 96.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F22 at OfficeLevel22::office_zone_2_landing {location = 100.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F23 at OfficeLevel23::office_zone_2_landing {location = 105.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F24 at OfficeLevel24::office_zone_2_landing {location = 109.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F25 at OfficeLevel25::office_zone_2_landing {location = 113.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F26 at OfficeLevel26::office_zone_2_landing {location = 117.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F27 at OfficeLevel27::office_zone_2_landing {location = 121.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F28 at OfficeLevel28::office_zone_2_landing {location = 126.0, departureRate = 0.04000} requires TradingFloorAccess on Arrive }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"3. OfficeExpress","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-express","text":"Runs directly to the Office Level 30 sky lobby from the lobby and Level 5. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 7.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.2 metres per second squared, duty 1800, and capacity 24. OfficeExpress.ttr transport OfficeExpress is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 7.0, acceleration = 1.2, duty = 1800, capacity = 24, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Office sky-lobby express elevators\" station F1 at MainLobby::office_express_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.45000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::office_express_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.15000} station F30 at OfficeSkyLobby30::office_express_landing {location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.40000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"4. OfficeZone3","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-zone3","text":"Distributes passengers from the Level 30 sky lobby to Office Levels 31–40. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 4.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 21. OfficeZone3.ttr transport OfficeZone3 is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 4.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 1600, capacity = 21, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Office Zone 3 elevators\" station F30 at OfficeSkyLobby30::office_zone_3_landing {location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.40000} station F31 at OfficeLevel31::office_zone_3_landing {location = 142.2, departureRate = 0.06000} station F32 at OfficeLevel32::office_zone_3_landing {location = 146.4, departureRate = 0.06000} station F33 at OfficeLevel33::office_zone_3_landing {location = 150.6, departureRate = 0.06000} station F34 at OfficeLevel34::office_zone_3_landing {location = 154.8, departureRate = 0.06000} station F35 at OfficeLevel35::office_zone_3_landing {location = 159.0, departureRate = 0.06000} station F36 at OfficeLevel36::office_zone_3_landing {location = 163.2, departureRate = 0.06000} station F37 at OfficeLevel37::office_zone_3_landing {location = 167.4, departureRate = 0.06000} station F38 at OfficeLevel38::office_zone_3_landing {location = 171.6, departureRate = 0.06000} station F39 at OfficeLevel39::office_zone_3_landing {location = 175.8, departureRate = 0.06000} station F40 at OfficeLevel40::office_zone_3_landing {location = 180.0, departureRate = 0.06000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"5. OfficeZone4","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-zone4","text":"Distributes passengers from the Level 30 sky lobby to Office Levels 40–50. Level 40 overlaps OfficeZone3. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 5.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 21. OfficeZone4.ttr transport OfficeZone4 is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 5.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 1600, capacity = 21, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Office Zone 4 elevators\" station F30 at OfficeSkyLobby30::office_zone_4_landing {location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.35000} station F40 at OfficeLevel40::office_zone_4_landing {location = 180.0, departureRate = 0.10000} station F41 at OfficeLevel41::office_zone_4_landing {location = 184.2, departureRate = 0.05500} station F42 at OfficeLevel42::office_zone_4_landing {location = 188.4, departureRate = 0.05500} station F43 at OfficeLevel43::office_zone_4_landing {location = 192.6, departureRate = 0.05500} station F44 at OfficeLevel44::office_zone_4_landing {location = 196.8, departureRate = 0.05500} station F45 at OfficeLevel45::office_zone_4_landing {location = 201.0, departureRate = 0.05500} station F46 at OfficeLevel46::office_zone_4_landing {location = 205.2, departureRate = 0.05500} station F47 at OfficeLevel47::office_zone_4_landing {location = 209.4, departureRate = 0.05500} station F48 at OfficeLevel48::office_zone_4_landing {location = 213.6, departureRate = 0.05500} station F49 at OfficeLevel49::office_zone_4_landing {location = 217.8, departureRate = 0.05500} station F50 at OfficeLevel50::office_zone_4_landing {location = 222.0, departureRate = 0.05500} requires TradingFloorAccess on Arrive }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"6. HotelExpress","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#hotel-express","text":"A four-car, high-speed shuttle between the main lobby and Hotel Lobby 55. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 10.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.2 metres per second squared, duty 1800, and capacity 24. HotelExpress.ttr transport HotelExpress is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 10.0, acceleration = 1.2, duty = 1800, capacity = 24, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Hotel express elevators\" station F1 at MainLobby::hotel_express_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.50000} station F55 at HotelLobby55::hotel_express_landing {location = 244.4, departureRate = 0.50000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"7. HotelLocal","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#hotel-local","text":"Serves every hotel floor, including Hotel Lobby 55. The selected parameters use 6 cars, a maximum velocity of 5.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 18. HotelLocal.ttr transport HotelLocal is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 5.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 1600, capacity = 18, carAmount = 6 } let displayName = \"Hotel local elevators\" station F52 at HotelLevel52::hotel_local_landing {location = 233.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F53 at HotelLevel53::hotel_local_landing {location = 236.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F54 at HotelLevel54::hotel_local_landing {location = 240.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F55 at HotelLobby55::hotel_local_landing {location = 244.4, departureRate = 0.28000} station F56 at HotelLevel56::hotel_local_landing {location = 248.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F57 at HotelLevel57::hotel_local_landing {location = 252.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F58 at HotelLevel58::hotel_local_landing {location = 255.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F59 at HotelLevel59::hotel_local_landing {location = 259.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F60 at HotelLevel60::hotel_local_landing {location = 263.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F61 at HotelLevel61::hotel_local_landing {location = 267.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F62 at HotelLevel62::hotel_local_landing {location = 271.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F63 at HotelLevel63::hotel_local_landing {location = 274.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F64 at HotelLevel64::hotel_local_landing {location = 278.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F65 at HotelLevel65::hotel_local_landing {location = 282.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F66 at HotelLevel66::hotel_local_landing {location = 286.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F67 at HotelLevel67::hotel_local_landing {location = 290.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F68 at HotelLevel68::hotel_local_landing {location = 293.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F69 at HotelLevel69::hotel_local_landing {location = 297.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F70 at HotelLevel70::hotel_local_landing {location = 301.4, departureRate = 0.04000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"8. ObservatoryExpress","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#observatory-express","text":"A four-car, very-high-speed service from Level 5 to Observatory Levels 72 and 73. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 12.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.3 metres per second squared, duty 2000, and capacity 24. ObservatoryExpress.ttr transport ObservatoryExpress is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 12.0, acceleration = 1.3, duty = 2000, capacity = 24, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Observatory express elevators\" station F5 at SharedLevel5::observatory_express_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.45000} station F72 at ObservatoryLevel72::observatory_express_landing {location = 313.4, departureRate = 0.30000} station F73 at ObservatoryLevel73::observatory_express_landing {location = 318.9, departureRate = 0.25000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"9. ObservatoryLocal","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#observatory-local","text":"A single-car local service connecting all four observatory levels. The selected parameters use 1 car, a maximum velocity of 3.0 metres per second, acceleration of 0.8 metres per second squared, duty 1400, and capacity 18. ObservatoryLocal.ttr transport ObservatoryLocal is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 3.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 1400, capacity = 18, carAmount = 1 } let displayName = \"Observatory local elevator\" station F72 at ObservatoryLevel72::observatory_local_landing {location = 313.4, departureRate = 0.25000} station F73 at ObservatoryLevel73::observatory_local_landing {location = 318.9, departureRate = 0.25000} station F74 at ObservatoryLevel74::observatory_local_landing {location = 324.4, departureRate = 0.25000} station F75 at ObservatoryLevel75::observatory_local_landing {location = 329.9, departureRate = 0.25000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"10. MainBuildingCarpark","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#main-building-carpark","text":"Links both carpark levels with the main lobby and the Level 5 tower–mall connection. The selected parameters use 3 cars, a maximum velocity of 3.0 metres per second, acceleration of 0.8 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 18. MainBuildingCarpark.ttr transport MainBuildingCarpark is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 3.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 1600, capacity = 18, carAmount = 3 } let displayName = \"Main-building carpark elevators\" station B3 at CarparkB3::main_carpark_landing {location = -12.0, departureRate = 0.20000} station B2 at CarparkB2::main_carpark_landing {location = -8.0, departureRate = 0.20000} station F1 at MainLobby::main_carpark_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.35000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::main_carpark_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.25000} }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"11. MallMain","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#mall-main","text":"Serves the carparks and every mall level. Mall Level 7 remains a mall-only map. The selected parameters use 4 cars, a maximum velocity of 2.5 metres per second, acceleration of 0.8 metres per second squared, duty 1600, and capacity 18. MallMain.ttr transport MallMain is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.5, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 1600, capacity = 18, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Mall passenger elevators\" station B3 at CarparkB3::mall_main_landing {location = -12.0, departureRate = 0.08000} station B2 at CarparkB2::mall_main_landing {location = -8.0, departureRate = 0.08000} station F1 at MainLobby::mall_main_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.20000} station F2 at MallLevel2::mall_main_landing {location = 6.0, departureRate = 0.12000} station F3 at MallLevel3::mall_main_landing {location = 12.0, departureRate = 0.12000} station F4 at MallLevel4::mall_main_landing {location = 18.0, departureRate = 0.12000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::mall_main_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.14000} station F6 at MallLevel6::mall_main_landing {location = 30.0, departureRate = 0.08000} station F7 at MallLevel7::mall_main_landing {location = 36.0, departureRate = 0.06000} ride-from any to MallDomain requires InMallOperationHours ride-from any to F5 requires InMallOperationHours }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"12. MallFreight","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#mall-freight","text":"A single-car, deliberately slow, heavy-duty service from B3 through Mall Level 6. The selected parameters use 1 car, a maximum velocity of 0.8 metres per second, acceleration of 0.4 metres per second squared, duty 5000, and capacity 40. MallFreight.ttr transport MallFreight is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 0.8, acceleration = 0.4, duty = 5000, capacity = 40, carAmount = 1 } let displayName = \"Mall freight elevator\" station B3 at CarparkB3::mall_freight_landing {location = -12.0, departureRate = 0.15000} station B2 at CarparkB2::mall_freight_landing {location = -8.0, departureRate = 0.15000} station B1 at LogisticsB1::mall_freight_landing {location = -4.0, departureRate = 0.15000} station F1 at MainLobby::mall_freight_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.10000} station F2 at MallLevel2::mall_freight_landing {location = 6.0, departureRate = 0.09000} station F3 at MallLevel3::mall_freight_landing {location = 12.0, departureRate = 0.09000} station F4 at MallLevel4::mall_freight_landing {location = 18.0, departureRate = 0.09000} station F5 at SharedLevel5::mall_freight_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.09000} station F6 at MallLevel6::mall_freight_landing {location = 30.0, departureRate = 0.09000} ride-from any to MallDomain requires InMallOperationHours ride-from any to F5 requires InMallOperationHours }"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"13. FireService","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#fire-service","text":"Two operator-guarded cars serving every level from B3 through Level 75. The selected parameters use 2 cars, a maximum velocity of 6.0 metres per second, acceleration of 1.0 metres per second squared, duty 2500, and capacity 24. FireService.ttr transport FireService is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 6.0, acceleration = 1.0, duty = 2500, capacity = 24, carAmount = 2 } let displayName = \"Fire-service elevators\" station B3 at CarparkB3::fire_service_landing {location = -12.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station B2 at CarparkB2::fire_service_landing {location = -8.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station B1 at LogisticsB1::fire_service_landing {location = -4.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F1 at MainLobby::fire_service_landing {location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F2 at MallLevel2::fire_service_landing {location = 6.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F3 at MallLevel3::fire_service_landing {location = 12.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F4 at MallLevel4::fire_service_landing {location = 18.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F5 at SharedLevel5::fire_service_landing {location = 24.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F6 at MallLevel6::fire_service_landing {location = 30.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F7 at MallLevel7::fire_service_landing {location = 36.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F8 at OfficeLevel8::fire_service_landing {location = 42.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F9 at OfficeLevel9::fire_service_landing {location = 46.2, departureRate = 0.01282} station F10 at OfficeLevel10::fire_service_landing {location = 50.4, departureRate = 0.01282} station F11 at OfficeLevel11::fire_service_landing {location = 54.6, departureRate = 0.01282} station F12 at OfficeLevel12::fire_service_landing {location = 58.8, departureRate = 0.01282} station F13 at OfficeLevel13::fire_service_landing {location = 63.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F14 at OfficeLevel14::fire_service_landing {location = 67.2, departureRate = 0.01282} station F15 at OfficeLevel15::fire_service_landing {location = 71.4, departureRate = 0.01282} station F16 at OfficeLevel16::fire_service_landing {location = 75.6, departureRate = 0.01282} station F17 at OfficeLevel17::fire_service_landing {location = 79.8, departureRate = 0.01282} station F18 at OfficeLevel18::fire_service_landing {location = 84.0, departureRate = 0.01282} station F19 at OfficeLevel19::fire_service_landing"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"14. OfficeServ","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#office-serv","text":"Two operator-guarded service cars linking Logistics B1 with every office level. The selected parameters use 2 cars, a maximum velocity of 3.0 metres per second, acceleration of 0.8 metres per second squared, duty 3000, and capacity 30. OfficeServ.ttr transport OfficeServ is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 3.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 3000, capacity = 30, carAmount = 2 } let displayName = \"Office service elevators\" station B1 at LogisticsB1::office_service_landing {location = -4.0, departureRate = 0.14000} station F8 at OfficeLevel8::office_service_landing {location = 42.0, departureRate = 0.02000} station F9 at OfficeLevel9::office_service_landing {location = 46.2, departureRate = 0.02000} station F10 at OfficeLevel10::office_service_landing {location = 50.4, departureRate = 0.02000} station F11 at OfficeLevel11::office_service_landing {location = 54.6, departureRate = 0.02000} station F12 at OfficeLevel12::office_service_landing {location = 58.8, departureRate = 0.02000} station F13 at OfficeLevel13::office_service_landing {location = 63.0, departureRate = 0.02000} station F14 at OfficeLevel14::office_service_landing {location = 67.2, departureRate = 0.02000} station F15 at OfficeLevel15::office_service_landing {location = 71.4, departureRate = 0.02000} station F16 at OfficeLevel16::office_service_landing {location = 75.6, departureRate = 0.02000} station F17 at OfficeLevel17::office_service_landing {location = 79.8, departureRate = 0.02000} station F18 at OfficeLevel18::office_service_landing {location = 84.0, departureRate = 0.02000} station F19 at OfficeLevel19::office_service_landing {location = 88.2, departureRate = 0.02000} station F20 at OfficeLevel20::office_service_landing {location = 92.4, departureRate = 0.02000} station F21 at OfficeLevel21::office_service_landing {location = 96.6, departureRate = 0.02000} station F22 at OfficeLevel22::office_service_landing {location = 100.8, departureRate = 0.02000} station F23 at OfficeLevel23::office_service_landing {location = 105.0, departureRate = 0.02000} station F24 at OfficeLevel24::office_service_landing {location = 109.2, departureRate = 0.02000} station F25 at OfficeLevel25::office_service_landing {location = 113.4, departureRate = 0.02000} station F26 at OfficeLevel26::office_service_landing {location = 117.6, departureRate = 0.02000} station F27 at OfficeLevel27::office_service_landing "},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"15. HotelServExpress","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#hotel-serv-express","text":"Two unrestricted service cars linking B2, B1, the Level 51 service/refuge floor, and every hotel floor. The selected parameters use 2 cars, a maximum velocity of 4.0 metres per second, acceleration of 0.8 metres per second squared, duty 3000, and capacity 30. HotelServExpress.ttr transport HotelServExpress is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 4.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 3000, capacity = 30, carAmount = 2 } let displayName = \"Hotel service express elevators\" station B2 at CarparkB2::hotel_service_express_landing {location = -8.0, departureRate = 0.10000} station B1 at LogisticsB1::hotel_service_express_landing {location = -4.0, departureRate = 0.10000} station F51 at ServiceRefugeLevel51::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 228.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F52 at HotelLevel52::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 233.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F53 at HotelLevel53::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 236.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F54 at HotelLevel54::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 240.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F55 at HotelLobby55::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 244.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F56 at HotelLevel56::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 248.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F57 at HotelLevel57::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 252.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F58 at HotelLevel58::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 255.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F59 at HotelLevel59::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 259.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F60 at HotelLevel60::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 263.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F61 at HotelLevel61::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 267.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F62 at HotelLevel62::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 271.0, departureRate = 0.04000} station F63 at HotelLevel63::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 274.8, departureRate = 0.04000} station F64 at HotelLevel64::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 278.6, departureRate = 0.04000} station F65 at HotelLevel65::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 282.4, departureRate = 0.04000} station F66 at HotelLevel66::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 286.2, departureRate = 0.04000} station F67 at HotelLevel67::hotel_service_express_landing {location = 290"},{"page":"SkyrimTower Mock Building","title":"16. HotelServLocal","href":"examples/skyrim-tower.html#hotel-serv-local","text":"One unrestricted local service car covering Level 51 and every hotel floor. The selected parameters use 1 car, a maximum velocity of 2.5 metres per second, acceleration of 0.7 metres per second squared, duty 2500, and capacity 24. HotelServLocal.ttr transport HotelServLocal is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.5, acceleration = 0.7, duty = 2500, capacity = 24, carAmount = 1 } let displayName = \"Hotel service local elevator\" station F51 at ServiceRefugeLevel51::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 228.0, departureRate = 0.05000} station F52 at HotelLevel52::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 233.0, departureRate = 0.05000} station F53 at HotelLevel53::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 236.8, departureRate = 0.05000} station F54 at HotelLevel54::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 240.6, departureRate = 0.05000} station F55 at HotelLobby55::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 244.4, departureRate = 0.05000} station F56 at HotelLevel56::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 248.2, departureRate = 0.05000} station F57 at HotelLevel57::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 252.0, departureRate = 0.05000} station F58 at HotelLevel58::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 255.8, departureRate = 0.05000} station F59 at HotelLevel59::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 259.6, departureRate = 0.05000} station F60 at HotelLevel60::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 263.4, departureRate = 0.05000} station F61 at HotelLevel61::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 267.2, departureRate = 0.05000} station F62 at HotelLevel62::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 271.0, departureRate = 0.05000} station F63 at HotelLevel63::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 274.8, departureRate = 0.05000} station F64 at HotelLevel64::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 278.6, departureRate = 0.05000} station F65 at HotelLevel65::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 282.4, departureRate = 0.05000} station F66 at HotelLevel66::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 286.2, departureRate = 0.05000} station F67 at HotelLevel67::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 290.0, departureRate = 0.05000} station F68 at HotelLevel68::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 293.8, departureRate = 0.05000} station F69 at HotelLevel69::hotel_service_local_landing {location = 297.6, departureRate = 0.05000} station F70 at HotelLevel70::hotel_se"},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Glossary","href":"glossary.html","text":"Glossary This glossary gives short definitions of TopoScript terms and links to the clauses that define their full behavior. It summarizes existing rules and does not introduce new ones."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"A","href":"glossary.html#letter-a","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Access constraint","href":"glossary.html#term-access-constraint","text":"A Boolean condition attached with requires that determines whether a path, station use, or transport ride participates in the compiled routing model. See Hard Access Constraints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Annotation","href":"glossary.html#term-annotation","text":"Data attached to a node or path. Recognized fields may affect filtering or route output; other fields have application-defined meaning. See Node Properties and Path Properties ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"any","href":"glossary.html#term-any","text":"A ride-from operand that matches every station or management domain at that operand position. See Ride Policies ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Application-defined","href":"glossary.html#term-application-defined","text":"Behavior whose meaning is intentionally left to the application consuming TopoNavi Engine output, especially custom annotations. See Stability Boundaries ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Arrive","href":"glossary.html#term-arrive","text":"A station-access qualifier indicating that a requirement applies when arriving at the station. See Station Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Atomic path","href":"glossary.html#term-atomic-path","text":"A direct, directed or bidirectional connection between two nodes in the same topo map that does not cross another intermediate node. It is declared with atomic-path . See Atomicity ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"at","href":"glossary.html#term-at","text":"The station-declaration connector between a station label and its qualified landing-node identifier. See Stations and Landing Nodes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"B","href":"glossary.html#letter-b","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Base map","href":"glossary.html#term-base-map","text":"A reusable topo-map source named after using when constructing a map instance. See Reusable Submap Instances ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Bool","href":"glossary.html#term-bool","text":"The primitive Boolean type containing true and false . See Primitive Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Building","href":"glossary.html#term-building","text":"The compiled collection of selected map graphs, transports, domains, and shared declarations that together describe one routable venue. See Relationship to the Compiled Building ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"building-includes","href":"glossary.html#term-building-includes","text":"The mandatory project manifest that selects map instances and transports for one compiled building. See Whole-Building Composition ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"because","href":"glossary.html#term-because","text":"An optional subject-to clause introducing a human-readable reason for uncertain access. See Uncertain Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"C","href":"glossary.html#letter-c","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Climbing","href":"glossary.html#term-climbing","text":"The route-step category used for stair traversal. See Stair Traversal Time and Transport and Climbing Step Fields ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Compiled building model","href":"glossary.html#term-compiled-building-model","text":"The navigation graphs and transport graph produced from a valid TopoScript project and its supplied compilation inputs. See Compiled Routing Model ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Compilation","href":"glossary.html#term-compilation","text":"The process that parses, elaborates, evaluates, and links project sources into a building model. See Language Lifecycle ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Compilation input","href":"glossary.html#term-compilation-input","text":"An external value supplied for a root parameter while compiling a project. See Compilation Inputs ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Constraint","href":"glossary.html#term-constraint","text":"A named set of Boolean requirements declared with constraint . Multiple require clauses form a conjunction. See Global Constraints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Cost","href":"glossary.html#term-cost","text":"A non-negative traversal-time weight used when comparing routes. Atomic-path costs and transport costs are measured in seconds. See Route-Cost Composition ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"costSeconds","href":"glossary.html#term-cost-seconds","text":"The route-output field containing one step's traversal time in seconds. See Cost, Duration, and Distance ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"D","href":"glossary.html#letter-d","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Disconnected ( DC )","href":"glossary.html#term-dc","text":"An RCC relationship in which two physical node areas share no physical point. See Node Physical Semantics ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"def","href":"glossary.html#term-def","text":"A declaration of a reusable function or calculated value. Root-level definitions are visible to selected maps and transports. See Global Functions ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Depart","href":"glossary.html#term-depart","text":"A station-access qualifier indicating that a requirement applies when departing from the station. See Station Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Directional arrow","href":"glossary.html#term-directional-arrow","text":"A directional-arrow declaration describing a target node's direction relative to the facing direction established at an anchor node. See Turn Hints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Domain","href":"glossary.html#term-domain","text":"Short form for management domain when discussing map grouping and ride policies. It is not a network or internet domain. See Management Domains ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"E","href":"glossary.html#letter-e","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Externally connected ( EC )","href":"glossary.html#term-ec","text":"An RCC relationship in which two physical node areas touch at their boundaries but their interiors do not intersect. See Node Physical Semantics ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Edge","href":"glossary.html#term-edge","text":"A directed connection in a compiled graph. An atomic path produces one edge, or two opposing edges when declared bidirectionally. See Compiled Routing Model ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Elaboration","href":"glossary.html#term-elaboration","text":"The compilation phase that resolves names, evaluates declarations and constraints, and converts surface-language declarations into compiled values. See Validation Phases ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Elevator","href":"glossary.html#term-elevator","text":"A multi-station transport whose cost is estimated from waiting and vertical movement time. See Elevators ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Escalator","href":"glossary.html#term-escalator","text":"A two-station fixed-time transport currently modeled as traversable in both directions. See Escalators ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Expression","href":"glossary.html#term-expression","text":"Source text that evaluates to a value, including literals, names, function applications, operators, records, lists, and conditional expressions. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Experimental","href":"glossary.html#term-experimental","text":"Behavior that may change substantially or be removed, currently including coordinate estimation from spatial relationships. See Stability Boundaries ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"F","href":"glossary.html#letter-f","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"fix","href":"glossary.html#term-fix","text":"An expression-language form for explicitly introducing recursion through a fixed-point expression. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Float","href":"glossary.html#term-float","text":"The primitive floating-point type. TopoScript does not generally convert between Float and Int implicitly. See Primitive Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Floor","href":"glossary.html#term-floor","text":"The physical building level normally modeled by one topo map. A floor name in prose should not be confused with a map identifier unless the project deliberately uses the same name. See Topo-Map Responsibilities and Boundaries ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"fn","href":"glossary.html#term-fn","text":"A keyword for writing an anonymous function. The backslash form is equivalent. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Function","href":"glossary.html#term-function","text":"A value that accepts an argument and produces another value. Function types use -> , and named functions are commonly declared with def . See Function Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"G","href":"glossary.html#letter-g","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Graph","href":"glossary.html#term-graph","text":"A compiled collection of nodes and directed edges used by routing. In API output, graph normally contains the identifier of the corresponding topo-map instance. See Waypoints and Identifiers ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"H","href":"glossary.html#letter-h","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Hard access","href":"glossary.html#term-hard-access","text":"An access condition that removes an unsatisfied path, station permission, or ride from the compiled routing model. Contrast with uncertain access. See Hard Access Constraints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"High-rise routing","href":"glossary.html#term-high-rise-routing","text":"A transport-first routing strategy selected with isHighRise=true . It is a strategy name, not a test of the building's physical height. See High-Rise Routing ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"I","href":"glossary.html#letter-i","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Identifier","href":"glossary.html#term-identifier","text":"A case-sensitive source name beginning with a letter or underscore and followed by letters, digits, or underscores. See Identifiers and Qualified Names ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"if / then / else","href":"glossary.html#term-if","text":"The expression-language conditional form. Its condition must be Boolean, and only the selected branch is evaluated. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Implementation-defined","href":"glossary.html#term-implementation-defined","text":"Behavior selected by TopoNavi Engine rather than fixed by TopoScript source semantics, including pathfinding and route-preference handling. See Stability Boundaries ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Int","href":"glossary.html#term-int","text":"The primitive signed-integer type. See Primitive Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"isHighRise","href":"glossary.html#term-is-high-rise","text":"A route-request selector choosing high-rise or fully informed low-rise routing. It does not describe a TopoScript declaration or physical-height threshold. See Building-Routing Modes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"is","href":"glossary.html#term-is","text":"The transport-declaration connector between a transport identifier and its kind, as in transport MainLift is Elevator . See Common Transport Declaration Model ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"L","href":"glossary.html#letter-l","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Landing node","href":"glossary.html#term-landing-node","text":"An existing node referenced by a transport station as the physical endpoint of that transport on a map. See Stations and Landing Nodes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"let","href":"glossary.html#term-let","text":"A local value binding. The expression form let name = value in body limits the name to the body; declaration forms may also appear inside supported source bodies. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Linear path","href":"glossary.html#term-linear-path","text":"A linear-path hint listing nodes in their physical order along one straight walking line. It groups route output but does not create connectivity. See Straight-Line Hints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"List","href":"glossary.html#term-list","text":"An ordered value containing elements of one inferred type. See Composite Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Low-rise routing","href":"glossary.html#term-low-rise-routing","text":"The fully informed building-wide routing strategy selected with isHighRise=false . The name denotes an algorithmic mode rather than a physical-height test. See Fully Informed Low-Rise Routing ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"M","href":"glossary.html#letter-m","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"managed-by","href":"glossary.html#term-managed-by","text":"A building-includes clause assigning a management domain to one configured map instance. See Management-Domain Attachment ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Management domain","href":"glossary.html#term-management-domain","text":"A named grouping of maps, usually based on the party responsible for managing access. Ride policies may use domain names as operands. See Management Domains ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Map","href":"glossary.html#term-map","text":"Short form for topo map when referring to a modeled floor and its local topology. It does not mean a metric drawing or coordinate plan. See Topo Maps ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Map instance","href":"glossary.html#term-map-instance","text":"A named map included in a building, either directly or constructed from a reusable base with using . Transports and route requests use the instance name. See Reusable Submap Instances ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Metadata","href":"glossary.html#term-metadata","text":"Descriptive or application-facing data attached to compiled entities. Metadata does not affect routing unless a field is explicitly recognized by TopoNavi Engine."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"N","href":"glossary.html#letter-n","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Navigation graph","href":"glossary.html#term-navigation-graph","text":"The compiled directed graph for one topo map, formed from its nodes and atomic paths. See Compiled Routing Model ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Node","href":"glossary.html#term-node","text":"A named topological entity representing one unambiguous, physically bounded, non-overlapping area of a venue. Source declarations use topo-node . See Nodes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"No-route outcome","href":"glossary.html#term-no-route","text":"A routing result indicating that no complete eligible path connects the requested origin and destination. It is not a partial route. See No-Route Outcomes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"O","href":"glossary.html#letter-o","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"out-of-order","href":"glossary.html#term-out-of-order","text":"A station marker that disables both arrival and departure for an elevator or escalator station. See Station Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"on","href":"glossary.html#term-on","text":"A station-annotation connector used with Arrive or Depart to limit which direction of station use an access condition affects. See Station Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"override-managed-by","href":"glossary.html#term-override-managed-by","text":"A topo-map declaration clause specifying the map's own management-domain override. See Management-Domain Override ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"P","href":"glossary.html#letter-p","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Parameter","href":"glossary.html#term-parameter","text":"A named typed input to a root, map, function, or other parameterized declaration. Root parameters receive application-supplied compilation inputs. See Compilation Inputs ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"params","href":"glossary.html#term-params","text":"The conventional local record containing recognized map or transport parameters. Its required fields depend on the containing declaration. See Map Parameters and Transport Declarations ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Path","href":"glossary.html#term-path","text":"In source-language discussions, normally an atomic path between two nodes. In routing discussions, it may mean an entire selected route. Use atomic path or route when the distinction matters."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Path tag","href":"glossary.html#term-path-tag","text":"A canonical string in a path's tags annotation. Tags may participate in request-time traversal filtering. See Banned Tags ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Q","href":"glossary.html#letter-q","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Qualified identifier","href":"glossary.html#term-qualified-identifier","text":"A name containing its scope, such as Lobby::main_entrance . For nodes, the left part is the map-instance identifier and the right part is the node's local identifier. See Identifiers and Qualified Names ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"R","href":"glossary.html#letter-r","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Record","href":"glossary.html#term-record","text":"A value containing named fields, written with braces. Fields are read using projection such as params.capacity . See Composite Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"rec","href":"glossary.html#term-rec","text":"A modifier in a let rec expression that introduces an explicitly recursive binding. See Expressions and Evaluation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Region Connection Calculus (RCC)","href":"glossary.html#term-rcc","text":"The qualitative spatial framework used here to state that distinct node areas must be disconnected ( DC ) or externally connected ( EC ), never overlapping. See Node Physical Semantics ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"require","href":"glossary.html#term-require","text":"A clause inside a named constraint . Its expression must evaluate to Bool . See Global Constraints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Required action","href":"glossary.html#term-required-action","text":"An application-facing instruction attached to a path, such as tapping an access card or opening a door. It is reported but does not change route eligibility or cost. See Required Actions ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"requires","href":"glossary.html#term-requires","text":"An annotation attaching one or more named hard access constraints to a path, station use, or ride policy. Contrast with the singular require clause inside a constraint declaration."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"ride-from","href":"glossary.html#term-ride-from","text":"A directed policy controlling rides between station labels, management domains, or any . It is not a whitelist: unmatched rides remain permitted. See Ride Policies ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Risk preference","href":"glossary.html#term-risk-preference","text":"A request-time control deciding whether routes with uncertain access may be selected. See Risk Preferences ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"root","href":"glossary.html#term-root","text":"The optional project-global source declaration providing shared parameters, constraints, and functions. It does not create a navigable map. See The Root Source ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Route","href":"glossary.html#term-route","text":"A traversable ordered result consisting of walking edges and transport movements between an origin node and a destination node. See Route Entity ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Route-planning preference","href":"glossary.html#term-route-planning-preference","text":"A request-time choice controlling how eligible route candidates are ranked, such as MinimizeTime , MinimizeTransfers , or MinimizePhysicalDemands . See Primary Route-Planning Preferences ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Route step","href":"glossary.html#term-route-step","text":"One ordered consumer-facing segment of a route result, such as Walk , WalkStraight , Transport , or Climbing . See Step Order and Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"S","href":"glossary.html#letter-s","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Stairs","href":"glossary.html#term-stairs","text":"A cross-map transport whose cost represents climbing time. See Stairs ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Station","href":"glossary.html#term-station","text":"A labeled transport stop connecting one transport to one existing landing node in a map. See Stations and Landing Nodes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Station label","href":"glossary.html#term-station-label","text":"A name local to one transport declaration. Ride policies may use it as an operand; it is distinct from the map and landing-node identifiers. See Stations and Landing Nodes ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"String","href":"glossary.html#term-string","text":"The primitive character-sequence type represented by a double-quoted literal. See Primitive Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"subject-to","href":"glossary.html#term-subject-to","text":"An annotation recording uncertain access without removing the affected path or station use from the compiled model. An optional because clause supplies a human-readable reason. See Uncertain Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"submap","href":"glossary.html#term-submap","text":"A building-includes entry selecting or instantiating a topo map. Despite its name, it does not create a parent-child map hierarchy. See No Parent-Map Hierarchy ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"T","href":"glossary.html#letter-t","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Tag","href":"glossary.html#term-tag","text":"A canonical string classifying a map entity for application use or recognized traversal filtering. See the TopoScript Tagging Guide ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Topological","href":"glossary.html#term-topological","text":"Describing connectivity, adjacency, and navigation-relevant relationships without requiring metric coordinates or a geometrically accurate floor plan."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"to","href":"glossary.html#term-to","text":"The ride-from connector separating its directed source and destination operands. See Ride Policies ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Topo map","href":"glossary.html#term-topo-map","text":"A graph-like declaration representing the navigable topology of one particular floor. It contains nodes, atomic paths, and optional navigation hints. See Topo Maps ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"topo-node","href":"glossary.html#term-topo-node","text":"The source declaration for a node in a topo map. See Node Syntax ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"TopoScript","href":"glossary.html#term-toposcript","text":"The domain-specific language used to describe an indoor venue's routable topology, cross-map transportation, access conditions, and navigation-relevant metadata. See Category of System ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Transport","href":"glossary.html#term-transport","text":"A connection between multiple topo maps. Supported transport kinds are Elevator , Escalator , and Stairs . See Cross-Map Transportation ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Transport graph","href":"glossary.html#term-transport-graph","text":"The compiled graph connecting transport stations across otherwise independent map graphs. See Compiled Routing Model ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"true and false","href":"glossary.html#term-true-false","text":"The two literals of type Bool . See Primitive Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"type","href":"glossary.html#term-type","text":"A parser-supported named type declaration. Its intended long-term role at building-global scope remains under design review and is not part of the stable global surface. See Documented Global Declarations ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"U","href":"glossary.html#letter-u","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Uncertain access","href":"glossary.html#term-uncertain-access","text":"A condition indicating that a path or station use may not be available, while allowing risk-aware routing to decide whether it may participate. See Uncertain Access ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"using","href":"glossary.html#term-using","text":"A submap clause constructing a named map instance from a reusable base topo-map source. See Reusable Submap Instances ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"V","href":"glossary.html#letter-v","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"vehicle","href":"glossary.html#term-vehicle","text":"The current building-includes keyword that selects a transport source. In explanatory prose, the canonical term is transport . See Whole-Building Syntax ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Visiting mode","href":"glossary.html#term-visiting-mode","text":"An internal routing concept historically used for mode-specific path costs. It is not part of the stable request-facing TopoScript reference at present."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"W","href":"glossary.html#letter-w","text":""},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Waypoint","href":"glossary.html#term-waypoint","text":"A structured node record included in a route step. Its graph and nodeId fields identify the corresponding TopoScript node. See Waypoints, Identifiers, and Descriptions ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Walk","href":"glossary.html#term-walk","text":"A route-step type grouping one or more consecutive walking edges on the same map. See Step Order and Types ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"WalkStraight","href":"glossary.html#term-walk-straight","text":"A walking route step recognized as lying along one declared linear-path . See Spatial and Turn Hints ."},{"page":"Glossary","title":"Important Terminology Distinctions","href":"glossary.html#terminology-distinctions","text":"Node and point: a node represents a bounded physical area, not an infinitesimal coordinate point. Atomic path and route: an atomic path is one direct local connection; a route is the complete ordered journey. Topo map and floor plan: a topo map models navigation topology, not metric geometry or architectural drawing data. Map and graph: a topo map is the source-language declaration; a navigation graph is its compiled routing representation. Map and submap: submap is the manifest keyword for a configured map instance; it does not imply containment beneath a parent map. Transport and vehicle: transport is the canonical concept; vehicle is the current manifest keyword used to select a transport source. require and requires : require appears inside a constraint declaration; requires attaches a named constraint to another declaration. Hard and uncertain access: failed hard access removes an option; uncertain access remains available only according to risk preference and is disclosed in the result. Station label and landing node: a station label names a stop within one transport; a landing node names the physical area in its topo map. Floor and map identifier: floor labels are real-world descriptions; map identifiers are exact, case-sensitive source names."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Language and Execution Model","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html","text":"Language and Execution Model Status: Draft"},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Intended Audience","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#intended-audience","text":"The primary audience consists of venue owners, operators, facility managers, and other specifically authorized parties responsible for creating or maintaining an indoor navigation model. It also includes authorized surveyors and contributors who can observe and record the venue’s traversable topology without requiring a metric survey. A map author should possess sufficient authoritative knowledge or approved observations for the portion of the venue being mapped. The author or publishing organization is responsible for validating the accuracy, access conditions, and operational information it publishes. A secondary audience consists of software developers, tool builders, and engine contributors who integrate with TopoScript projects or implement authoring, validation, and navigation tools."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Prerequisite Knowledge","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#prerequisite-knowledge","text":"Basic map authors should understand locations, directed connections, access restrictions, and traversal costs. They should also be familiar with the part of the venue they are describing or have access to an authoritative source for it. Authors who use the advanced parts of TopoScript should additionally be familiar with typed values, expressions, functions, and parameters. Developers integrating the compiler or consuming its results should be familiar with structured data and graph-based route planning. Knowledge of GIS, CAD, metric surveying, Scala, or the internal implementation of the TopoNavi engine is not required to author a basic TopoScript project."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Category of System Described by TopoScript","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#system-category","text":"TopoScript is a domain-specific language for describing the routable topology of an indoor venue. It models named node areas, directed or bidirectional connections between those areas, traversal costs, reusable area maps, and transportation connections between maps or floors. TopoScript can associate typed metadata with topological entities and express access conditions using parameters and constraints. Such metadata may describe matters such as a location’s purpose, operational information, tags, or required actions. Metadata has routing behavior only where TopoNavi defines that behavior or where the author connects it to an evaluated constraint. For example, business hours may be stored as descriptive metadata. TopoNavi does not currently provide a built-in date, time, calendar, or business-hours model and does not automatically enforce descriptive business-hours metadata. Time-dependent access must be modeled explicitly through supported parameters and constraints. TopoScript describes logical reachability and route-planning relationships rather than a geometrically exact representation of the physical venue. Two node areas may be physically close without being connected by a traversable path, and a declared connection represents permitted topological movement rather than measured geometric adjacency."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Language Lifecycle","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#language-lifecycle","text":"A TopoScript project passes through four stages: Source declaration: authors describe the building configuration, shared parameters and constraints, maps, nodes, paths, and transports. Compilation: the engine parses and checks the sources, resolves declarations and supplied parameter values, evaluates TopoScript expressions and constraints, and produces a compiled building model containing navigation and transport graphs. Route planning: a caller queries the compiled model with an origin, a destination, and routing options such as route preference, banned tags, and uncertain-access handling. TopoScript expressions and constraints are not re-evaluated during this stage; a different compilation-parameter context requires a new compiled model. Navigation result: the engine returns either a route-planning error or an ordered route containing the selected nodes and traversals, together with applicable navigation hints, required actions, and access-uncertainty information. The source project defines what may be traversed, compilation creates the model for one parameter context, and route planning selects a route through that model for one request."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Major Language Entities and Their Relationships","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#major-language-entities","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Building","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#building-entity","text":"A building is the complete venue model produced by compiling one TopoScript project. It consists of a configured set of maps, transports connecting those maps, management-domain assignments, shared declarations, and compilation metadata. TopoScript does not currently contain a separate named building declaration. The building-includes source determines which maps and transports participate in the compiled building."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Map","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#map-entity","text":"A map is a named local topology containing nodes and atomic paths. It normally represents one floor, area, or other logical subdivision of a venue. Nodes inside a map share a local namespace. Atomic paths connect nodes within that map. Connections between maps are described separately by transport stations and transport declarations. A map is graph-like but is not required to contain one fully connected component."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Node","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#node-entity","text":"A node is a named topological entity representing one unambiguous, physically bounded area of a venue. A node may represent a destination area, entrance area, doorway area, junction area, corridor-corner area, transport-landing area, decision area, or another navigation-relevant region. The physical areas represented by any two distinct nodes must not overlap. Their spatial relationship must be one of: Disconnected ( DC ) : the two areas do not share any physical point. Externally connected ( EC ) : the boundaries of the two areas touch, but their interiors do not intersect. Distinct nodes must therefore not partially overlap, represent equal physical areas, or contain one another. If a physical region would otherwise be represented by overlapping nodes, the author must partition it into non-overlapping node areas. A node may participate in zero or more atomic paths and may be referenced by one or more transport stations. Its identifier is local to its containing map; outside that map, it is referenced using a qualified identifier such as MapName::NodeName ."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Atomic Path","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#atomic-path-entity","text":"An atomic path is a directed or bidirectional local connection between exactly two nodes in the same map. It represents a single indivisible traversal segment for routing purposes and carries a traversal cost and optional metadata or access conditions. An atomic path should not pass through another declared navigation-relevant node. If one physical passage connects A , B , and C in that order, the passage should normally be represented by A-B and B-C , not by one A-C atomic path. An additional A-C atomic path is appropriate only when it represents a separate direct traversal that does not require passing through B . TopoScript treats this as an authoring and data-quality responsibility; it does not geometrically detect intermediate or intersecting nodes."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Transport","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#transport-entity","text":"A transport describes a named mechanism for traveling between maps through a set of stations. Each station associates the transport with a node in a participating map. TopoScript currently supports the transport types Elevator , Escalator , and Stairs . A transport may define traversal parameters, station access conditions, uncertainty information, and origin-to-destination ride policies. A transport is distinct from an atomic path: an atomic path connects two nodes locally within one map, while a transport provides modeled traversal between station nodes belonging to different maps."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Station","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#station-entity","text":"A station is a transport-local stop associated with one qualified node in a map. It is the interface through which a route enters, leaves, or traverses a transport. A station has a label local to its transport and references its map node using MapName::NodeName . It may carry transport-specific data and may independently restrict departure, arrival, or both. A map node is a location in the venue topology; a station is a transport’s use of that location. Multiple transports may therefore refer to nodes on the same map without making those nodes themselves transports."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Management Domain","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#management-domain-entity","text":"A management domain is a named classification assigned to a map, usually representing the party, operational area, or access-policy authority responsible for that map. Management domains allow transport ride policies to describe access between groups of maps without enumerating every map or station pair. Transport policies may refer to a management domain, an exact station label, or any . A management domain is currently introduced by assigning its name to a map rather than through a separate domain declaration. Each compiled map has one effective management domain. A map-level override takes precedence over its configuration assignment, and an unassigned map uses the fallback domain Misc ."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Parameters and Constraints","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#parameter-and-constraint-entities","text":"A parameter is an externally supplied typed value used while compiling a TopoScript project. Parameters allow the compiled topology and access permissions to depend on information about the intended routing context or user. A constraint is a named collection of Boolean requirements evaluated against the available declarations and parameter values. Constraints can control whether atomic paths, transport stations, or transport rides are included or considered accessible. In the current engine, parameters and constraints participate in compilation. They are not independently re-evaluated for every route request made against an existing compilation result. A different parameter context may therefore require a separately compiled result."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Route","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#route-entity","text":"A route is an ordered navigation result connecting an origin node to a destination node under a particular compiled parameter context and route-planning request. Conceptually, a route alternates between visited nodes and traversals connecting those nodes. A traversal may correspond to an atomic walking path, a transport ride, a stair-climbing segment, or another engine-defined route-edge category. The runtime representation contains an ordered sequence of globally qualified nodes and a corresponding ordered sequence of route edges. It may additionally contain navigation hints, required actions, and access-uncertainty information. A returned route is expected to be traversable under the compiled hard constraints. When uncertain-access fallback is used, the route may contain explicitly disclosed segments whose availability could not be confirmed."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Relationship Summary","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#entity-relationship-summary","text":"A building project supplies shared parameters, constraints, and definitions. A building project contains maps and transports. A map belongs to one effective management domain. A map contains nodes and atomic paths between its local nodes. A transport contains stations. A station associates a transport with a qualified node in a map. A transport may apply station-based or management-domain-based ride policies. Compilation turns the project model into navigation and transport graphs. Routing turns the compiled graphs into an ordered route result."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Source-File and Project Boundaries","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#source-file-and-project-boundaries","text":"A TopoScript project is one independently compiled venue model composed of a building configuration, an optional root source, referenced topo-map sources, and referenced transport sources. The project may be supplied to the compiler as a filesystem directory or as an in-memory collection that maps filenames to source text."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Building configuration","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#source-file-and-project-boundaries","text":"The required configuration.tcfg or extensionless configuration source determines which maps and transports participate in the project."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Root source","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#source-file-and-project-boundaries","text":"The optional extensionless root source declares parameters, constraints, types, values, and functions shared with the project’s topo-map and transport sources."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Topo-map source","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#source-file-and-project-boundaries","text":"A referenced map named Name is loaded from Name.tmap or the extensionless source Name . A topo-map describes the locally connected topology of a floor, area, or other logical subdivision of the venue."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Transport source","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#source-file-and-project-boundaries","text":"A referenced vehicle named Name is loaded from Name.ttr or the extensionless source Name . A transport connects qualified nodes belonging to the project’s topo maps. A source belongs to one of the top-level categories building-includes , root , topo-map , or transport . Authors should place one complete top-level definition in each source. Additional trailing top-level definitions are unsupported. The building configuration may instantiate a reusable topo map with a submap Instance using Base declaration. The instance participates in the compiled project under the instance name while reusing the base topo-map definition. Root declarations form the shared environment used when topo maps and transports are elaborated. Transport sources may resolve qualified map-node references such as MapName::NodeName . Topo-map sources describe their own local nodes and connections; cross-map traversal is described by transport sources. The compilation project is the boundary of name resolution and graph construction. Sources from separate projects do not implicitly share declarations, maps, transports, or compiled graph state."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Lexical Structure","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#lexical-structure","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Case Sensitivity","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#case-sensitivity","text":"TopoScript is case-sensitive. Keywords, identifiers, and literal names must use their declared capitalization."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Whitespace and Line Breaks","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#whitespace-and-line-breaks","text":"Spaces and horizontal tabs separate tokens and are otherwise ignored. Line breaks are significant where the grammar uses them to separate declarations or elements; they are optional only where the applicable grammar rule permits them."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Comments","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#comments","text":"A line comment begins with // and continues to the end of the line. A block comment begins with /* and ends at the next */ . Block comments may span lines and do not nest. Comments are ignored during parsing."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Literals","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#literals","text":"An integer literal contains one or more decimal digits. A floating-point literal contains decimal digits, a decimal point, and further decimal digits. Exponent notation and omitted digits on either side of the point are not supported. A negative number is parsed as unary negation applied to a numeric literal. A Boolean literal is true or false . A string literal is enclosed in double quotation marks and cannot contain a physical line break. TopoScript does not currently define general-purpose escape-sequence interpretation."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Identifiers and Qualified Names","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#identifiers-and-qualified-names","text":"An identifier begins with an ASCII letter or underscore and continues with zero or more ASCII letters, decimal digits, or underscores. In grammar notation: [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* Identifiers are case-sensitive. Hyphens, spaces, punctuation, and non-ASCII letters are not permitted. Reserved TopoScript keywords cannot be used where the grammar expects an identifier. A simple identifier consists of one identifier segment, such as Lobby or elevator_hall . A qualified name joins identifier segments with :: , such as Floor1::elevator_hall . A transport station reference to a map node must contain exactly two segments: the map identifier followed by the node identifier. The period in an expression such as value.field denotes record-field projection and is not a qualified-name separator. The meaning and visibility of an identifier depend on the declaration and scope in which it appears. Qualification identifies a path through named entities; it does not import names from another TopoScript project."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Name Scopes and Visibility","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#name-scopes-and-visibility","text":"Visibility flows from the project root into its topo maps and transports. Parameters, constraints, types, values, and functions defined by the root are available while each child map and transport is compiled. Declarations made by a child are not visible to the root. Declarations inside a topo-map or transport body are local to that body. They may use visible root declarations, and a local declaration with the same name shadows the outer declaration. Map-local declarations are not automatically visible to sibling maps or transports, and transport-local declarations are not exported. Node identifiers belong to their containing map. Local topology declarations, including atomic paths and spatial annotations, may refer to nodes in that map by simple name. A transport refers to a map node by its qualified name. Station labels are local to their containing transport. A braced root, topo-map, or transport body establishes its declaration scope. A braced expression block establishes a nested lexical scope for its local let bindings. Function parameters, lambda parameters, let expressions, recursive bindings, and fixed-point bindings are visible only in their respective bodies. Not every pair of braces creates a name scope. Braces used for records, constraints, or building configuration group their contents but do not by themselves introduce general-purpose local bindings. Definitions in the same declaration body may refer to one another without relying on source order, provided their dependency graph is acyclic. Cyclic definition dependencies are rejected."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Primitive and Domain-Specific Types","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Primitive Types","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Int","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"A signed integer value."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Float","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"A floating-point value. TopoScript does not implicitly convert between Int and Float in general expressions."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Bool","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"One of the Boolean values true or false ."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"String","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"A sequence of characters represented by a string literal."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Composite Types","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"A record type associates named fields with types, as in {name: String, level: Int} . A record value must provide values compatible with its expected field types. A list contains values of one element type. The element type is inferred from the elements. The current surface language provides no element-type annotation for an empty list, so an empty list cannot be evaluated successfully. A function type is written Input -> Output . Function arrows associate to the right, so Int -> Int -> Bool means Int -> (Int -> Bool) ."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Domain-Specific Entities","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#types","text":"Nodes, maps, transports, stations, management domains, constraints, buildings, and routes are domain-specific TopoScript entities, but they are not currently first-class value types that can be written in a type expression. Elevator , Escalator , and Stairs are transport kinds rather than general-purpose types. Named type declarations and internal proposition or enumeration types are not yet consistently available through the surface language. They must not be treated as stable, user-defined domain types in the current reference version."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Literals, Expressions, Operators, and Evaluation","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Literals and Compound Values","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":"TopoScript provides integer, floating-point, Boolean, string, list, and record literals. List elements must have one common type. Record fields are evaluated as expressions and are accessed using field projection, such as params.capacity ."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Expressions","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":"A name evaluates to the value bound to that name in the current scope. if condition then a else b evaluates the condition and then evaluates only the selected branch. The condition must be Bool , and both branches must produce the same type. let name = value in body evaluates the value and makes it available in the body. A function may be applied as f value or f(value) . Multiple arguments are represented as curried applications. Lambdas, recursive bindings, fixed-point expressions, and braced expression blocks are supported. Recursion must be introduced explicitly. Functions use lexical scope and evaluate arguments before applying the function."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Operators","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":"Operator Operands Result -value Int or Float Numeric negation of the same type + , - , * Two Int values or two Float values A value of the same numeric type / Any pair of Int or Float values Float , including for two integer operands == Two compatible Int , Float , Bool , or String values Bool , > Two Int values or two Float values Bool Division converts integer operands to floating point before evaluation: 3 / 2 produces 1.5 , and 240 / 300 produces 0.8 . Division by 0 , 0.0 , or -0.0 fails with an arithmetic error. Nonfinite operands and results are also rejected. Calculations use double-precision floating point, so rounding can occur, including loss of integer precision beyond 2 53 . Mixed Int and Float addition, subtraction, and multiplication are not implicitly converted. The operators and >= appear in the current parser grammar but are not implemented by the surface-language evaluator and must not be used. General-purpose Boolean operators are not currently supported; && inside a grouped requires annotation denotes a conjunction of named requirements rather than an ordinary expression operator."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Precedence","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":"From highest to lowest, the principal precedence groups are atomic values and projection, function application, unary negation, multiplication and division, addition and subtraction, comparison, and control-flow expressions. Multiplication and division associate to the left: 12 / 3 / 2 means (12 / 3) / 2 . Parentheses may be used to make grouping explicit."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Evaluation Rules","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#expressions-and-evaluation","text":"TopoScript expressions are deterministic and have no general-purpose side effects. Evaluation is strict except that an if expression evaluates only its selected branch. References must resolve, function arguments must match parameter types, record fields must exist when projected, and operators require compatible operand types. Definitions in a root, map, or transport body are evaluated according to their dependencies rather than their textual order. Constraints are evaluated during compilation. Type annotations on let declarations and declared return types on def declarations are parsed but are not yet consistently enforced by the current implementation."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Stability Boundaries","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#stability-boundaries","text":""},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Implementation-Defined Routing Behavior","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#stability-boundaries","text":"TopoScript defines the compiled topology and access state. Path selection, accessibility-route calculation, uncertain-access handling, routing preferences, heuristics, and tie-breaking are defined by the TopoNavi Engine implementation rather than by the core language."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Application-Defined Annotation Semantics","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#stability-boundaries","text":"Custom node and path annotations are preserved as structured attributes and may be exposed as JSON-compatible values by an API. Except for reserved attributes recognized by TopoNavi Engine, their meaning and handling are determined by the consuming application."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Experimental Coordinate Estimation","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#stability-boundaries","text":"Coordinate estimation derives approximate spatial coordinates from topological and spatial-relation declarations. This facility is experimental; its inputs, algorithm, output, and availability may change significantly, and the facility may be removed if the approach does not prove reliable."},{"page":"Language and Execution Model","title":"Intended Scope, Non-Goals, and Safety Assumptions","href":"language/language-and-execution-model.html#scope-non-goals-and-safety","text":"TopoScript is intended for indoor route planning over modeled, pedestrian-scale paths and building transportation systems. It is not a road-navigation model, a surveying format, or a metric geometric source of truth. TopoScript does not require GIS data or metric coordinates. Coordinates estimated or inferred by the engine are derived navigation metadata and must not be treated as surveyed physical coordinates. TopoNavi has not been validated as an emergency-response, evacuation, or life-safety system. Its routes must not supersede official signage, emergency procedures, applicable accessibility requirements, or the judgment of first responders and responsible venue personnel. Map authors and publishers are responsible for obtaining appropriate authorization, establishing data provenance, maintaining map accuracy, representing access controls correctly, and ensuring that venue information is collected, published, and used lawfully."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Lexical Structure","href":"language/lexical-structure.html","text":"Lexical Structure Status: Draft"},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Case Sensitivity","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#case-sensitivity","text":"TopoScript is case-sensitive. Keywords, identifiers, and literal names must use their declared capitalization."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Whitespace and Line Breaks","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#whitespace-and-line-breaks","text":"Spaces and horizontal tabs separate tokens and are otherwise ignored. Line breaks are significant where the grammar uses them to separate declarations or elements; they are optional only where the applicable grammar rule permits them."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Comments","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#comments","text":"A line comment begins with // and continues to the end of the line. A block comment begins with /* and ends at the next */ . Block comments may span lines and do not nest. Comments are ignored during parsing."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Literals","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#literals","text":"An integer literal contains one or more decimal digits. A floating-point literal contains decimal digits, a decimal point, and further decimal digits. Exponent notation and omitted digits on either side of the point are not supported. A negative number is parsed as unary negation applied to a numeric literal. A Boolean literal is true or false . A string literal is enclosed in double quotation marks and cannot contain a physical line break. TopoScript does not currently define general-purpose escape-sequence interpretation."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Identifiers and Qualified Names","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#identifiers-and-qualified-names","text":"An identifier begins with an ASCII letter or underscore and continues with zero or more ASCII letters, decimal digits, or underscores. In grammar notation: [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* Identifiers are case-sensitive. Hyphens, spaces, punctuation, and non-ASCII letters are not permitted. Reserved TopoScript keywords cannot be used where the grammar expects an identifier. A simple identifier consists of one identifier segment, such as Lobby or elevator_hall . A qualified name joins identifier segments with :: , such as Floor1::elevator_hall . A transport station reference to a map node must contain exactly two segments: the map identifier followed by the node identifier. The period in an expression such as value.field denotes record-field projection and is not a qualified-name separator. The meaning and visibility of an identifier depend on the declaration and scope in which it appears. Qualification identifies a path through named entities; it does not import names from another TopoScript project."},{"page":"Lexical Structure","title":"Name Scopes and Visibility","href":"language/lexical-structure.html#name-scopes-and-visibility","text":"Visibility flows from the project root into its topo maps and transports. Parameters, constraints, types, values, and functions defined by the root are available while each child map and transport is compiled. Declarations made by a child are not visible to the root. Declarations inside a topo-map or transport body are local to that body. They may use visible root declarations, and a local declaration with the same name shadows the outer declaration. Map-local declarations are not automatically visible to sibling maps or transports, and transport-local declarations are not exported. Node identifiers belong to their containing map. Local topology declarations, including atomic paths and spatial annotations, may refer to nodes in that map by simple name. A transport refers to a map node by its qualified name. Station labels are local to their containing transport. A braced root, topo-map, or transport body establishes its declaration scope. A braced expression block establishes a nested lexical scope for its local let bindings. Function parameters, lambda parameters, let expressions, recursive bindings, and fixed-point bindings are visible only in their respective bodies. Not every pair of braces creates a name scope. Braces used for records, constraints, or building configuration group their contents but do not by themselves introduce general-purpose local bindings. Definitions in the same declaration body may refer to one another without relying on source order, provided their dependency graph is acyclic. Cyclic definition dependencies are rejected."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html","text":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters TopoNavi separates access conditions that shape the compiled building from request-time controls that filter or rank routes through that building."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Compilation Inputs and Route Inputs","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#compilation-and-route-inputs","text":"User parameters are compilation inputs. They determine the user's access and capability context while the building is compiled: { \"userParams\": { \"officeAccess\": true, \"carryingWeight\": 20, \"timeOfDayInSeconds\": 50400 } } Risk preference, banned tags, and route-planning preference are request-time traversal controls: { \"traversalPreference\": { \"routePlanningPreference\": \"MinimizeTime\", \"banTags\": [\"outdoor\", \"rain_exposed\"], \"riskPreference\": \"conservative\" } } Both groups are supplied in the navigation request body. For the full request format, see the Navigation Request Guide . User parameters form part of the compiled-building cache identity. Changing them requires a matching compiled model. Traversal controls do not modify the compiled graph and may vary between route requests."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Hard Access Constraints","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#hard-access-constraints","text":"A failed requires condition is a hard prohibition. Depending on its attachment point, it may remove an atomic path, prohibit arrival at a station, prohibit departure from a station, prohibit both arrival and departure, or prohibit a directed transport ride. atomic-path [public_hall -> staff_corridor] { cost = 4 } requires StaffAccess When StaffAccess is false, the directed path is absent from the usable compiled topology. Hard constraints are evaluated during compilation. The route planner consumes their compiled effects and does not re-evaluate their expressions. See Compilation Time and Routing Time ."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Uncertain Access","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#uncertain-access","text":"A subject-to condition describes a traversal that may be unavailable but is not a definite prohibition. atomic-path [public_hall -> rooftop_garden] { cost = 12 } subject-to RooftopOpen because \"The rooftop may close during severe weather.\" If RooftopOpen is satisfied, the path is treated as ordinarily available. If it is unsatisfied, the path remains in the compiled model, conservative searches exclude it, permissive searches may select it, and a selected route exposes its uncertainty reason. Repeated failed uncertainty conditions of the same type are consolidated in route-level disclosure. A requires condition and a subject-to condition cannot be attached to the same element."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Risk Preferences","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#risk-preferences","text":"Placeholder: route selection under conservative, permissive, and aggressive uncertain-access preferences."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"conservative","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#risk-preferences","text":"The engine first searches without failed subject-to traversals. If a certain route exists, that route is returned. If no certain route exists, the engine retries with uncertain traversals enabled and reports permissive as the applied risk preference. Conservative routing therefore prefers a certain route but returns an uncertain route rather than failing when it is the only available option."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"permissive","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#risk-preferences","text":"The engine calculates the best route excluding failed uncertain access and the best route allowing it. The uncertain route is selected only when it meaningfully improves the primary route objective: Primary preference Required improvement MinimizeTime At least 15 seconds faster. MinimizeTransfers At least one fewer non-walking segment. MinimizePhysicalDemands At least a 10% lower physical-demand score. If no certain route exists, the uncertain route is returned."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"aggressive","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#risk-preferences","text":"All uncertain traversals are admitted into the search. The best resulting route is returned without comparison against a certain-only route. permissive and aggressive do not assert that access is lawful, safe, or physically available. Uncertainty descriptions originate from the map author and may be inaccurate or outdated."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Banned Tags","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#banned-tags","text":"banTags is a request-scoped hard filter over node and atomic-path tags. { \"traversalPreference\": { \"banTags\": [\"outdoor\", \"rain_exposed\"] } } A route cannot traverse an atomic path containing a banned tag or enter a node containing a banned tag. Node and path tags are evaluated independently; a node tag is not automatically inherited by its incident paths. Endpoint behavior is: A source node may contain a banned tag because the user is already there. Outgoing paths from that source are still checked. A destination containing a banned tag produces DESTINATION_HAS_BANNED_TAG . An intermediate node containing a banned tag cannot be entered. Tag matching is exact and case-sensitive. If tag filtering leaves no route, the service returns NO_ROUTE_WITH_BAN_TAGS . Transport rides do not carry traversable tags of their own. Tags on transport landing nodes can prevent entry into those landings. For tag-authoring conventions, see the TopoScript Tagging Guide . For request-body usage, see the Navigation Request Guide ."},{"page":"Constraints, Parameters, and Traversal Filters","title":"Required Actions and Other Annotations","href":"routing/constraints-and-parameters.html#required-actions","text":"Atomic-path annotations are preserved on returned walking segments. The annotation keys requiredActions , required_actions , and action_required are recognized as required-action lists. atomic-path [public_hall -> controlled_door] { cost = 5, requiredActions = [\"tap_access_card\", \"open_door\"] } Required actions are reported to the consuming application but do not affect route eligibility, cost, or ranking. The application is responsible for presenting or handling them. Other custom path annotations are not interpreted as routing rules unless explicitly documented."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Routing Modes and Preferences","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html","text":"Routing Modes and Preferences A routing mode determines how the building is searched. A route-planning preference determines how eligible transport candidates are ranked within that search."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Request-Body Controls","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#request-body-controls","text":"routePlanningPreference , riskPreference , and banTags are fields of the request body's traversalPreference object. Compilation parameters are fields of userParams . { \"userParams\": { \"officeAccess\": true }, \"traversalPreference\": { \"routePlanningPreference\": \"MinimizeTime\", \"riskPreference\": \"conservative\", \"banTags\": [\"outdoor\"] } } For the full HTTP request format and examples, see the Navigation Request Guide ."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Order of Route Decisions","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#decision-order","text":"Placeholder: compiled access, request filters, routing mode, preference ranking, and output annotation. Compilation parameters establish the building's hard-access topology. Banned tags exclude request-specific nodes and paths. The risk preference determines whether failed uncertain access may participate. isHighRise selects the building-routing strategy. The route-planning preference ranks eligible candidates. Output annotations describe the selected route. A route preference cannot restore a path, station, or ride removed by a hard access constraint."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Building-Routing Modes","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#building-routing-modes","text":""},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"isHighRise=false","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#building-routing-modes","text":"Uses fully informed routing, which compares complete walking and transport costs throughout the building."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"isHighRise=true","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#building-routing-modes","text":"Uses transport-first routing, which ranks cross-map transport plans before connecting their floor-level walking legs. The quick-demo endpoint defaults to isHighRise=true . The flag selects an algorithm and does not describe or validate the building's literal height. See Fully Informed Low-Rise Routing and High-Rise Routing ."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Primary Route-Planning Preferences","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#primary-preferences","text":"The supported values are MinimizeTime , MinimizeTransfers , and MinimizePhysicalDemands . The default is MinimizeTime . Unknown values are rejected by the web API."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"MinimizeTime","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#minimize-time","text":"Routes are ranked using their calculated traversal costs. In fully informed low-rise mode, this preference minimizes complete end-to-end cost. In high-rise mode, it initially minimizes the transport portion of the journey. Walking to and from the selected transport is included in the returned route but not in the initial transport ranking."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"MinimizeTransfers","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#minimize-transfers","text":"In high-rise mode, candidates are ordered first by the number of edges in the transport plan and then by total transport-plan cost. The counted edges include both cross-map rides and same-floor connections between transport lines. The value is therefore a transport-plan hop count, not strictly the number of times a passenger changes vehicles. In low-rise mode, this preference currently produces the same total-cost search as MinimizeTime ."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"MinimizePhysicalDemands","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#minimize-physical-demands","text":"High-rise transport candidates use the following relative score: elevator movement: 0.1 × vertical distance ; stair movement: 10 × vertical distance ; escalator movement: fixed travel time; and walking between transport landings: walking time. Lower scores are preferred. Origin and destination walking legs are not part of the initial high-rise transport score. This score is a routing heuristic, not a physiological, medical, energy-expenditure, or accessibility measurement. In low-rise mode, this preference currently produces the same total-cost search as MinimizeTime . Placeholder: the same route candidates ranked by time, transport-plan hops, and physical-demand score."},{"page":"Routing Modes and Preferences","title":"Preference Summary","href":"routing/preferences-and-modes.html#preference-summary","text":"Preference Fully informed low-rise mode High-rise mode MinimizeTime Minimizes complete route cost. Ranks the transport plan by cost before floor-level walking is connected. MinimizeTransfers Currently minimizes complete route cost. Ranks by transport-plan edge count, then transport cost. MinimizePhysicalDemands Currently minimizes complete route cost. Ranks by the transport physical-demand heuristic."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","href":"routing/route-results.html","text":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs TopoNavi Engine compiles a TopoScript project into a building model and uses that model to produce an ordered route plan. Applications commonly receive a JSON representation of the route plan through the TopoNavi web service. This article defines the relationship between TopoScript declarations and the current consumer-visible output. HTTP status codes, cache metadata, and service-specific response envelopes are API concerns rather than TopoScript language semantics."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Output Boundary","href":"routing/route-results.html#output-boundary","text":"The compiler's internal result contains per-map navigation graphs, a transport graph, map ordering, spatial hints, and compiler metadata. These internal Scala objects are not a portable serialization format and are not a public JSON contract. The supported consumer-facing representation is a route projected into ordered structured steps and waypoint records. REST, MCP, editor, and agent integrations may wrap or transform that representation. Such envelopes do not change the meaning of a node , path , topo map , or transport ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Compiled-Building Views","href":"routing/route-results.html#compiled-building-views","text":"A compiled building contains the map graphs and connecting transport graph described by Relationship to the Compiled Building . The current web service provides convenience views for: available maps ; available nodes , with optional node attributes; compiled directed walking edges ; compiled elevators and their stations; one node's attributes; and nodes reachable near a supplied node. These are web-service projections of the compiled model, not additional TopoScript entities. Consumers must not serialize or persist the engine's internal graph objects as though they were a language-defined interchange format."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Successful Route Response","href":"routing/route-results.html#successful-route-response","text":"The current quick-demo REST endpoint returns the following top-level fields after a successful route request: Field Meaning status \"success\" for a successful web request. path A human-readable rendering of the route. It is for display and inspection, not machine parsing. steps The ordered structured route steps defined below. appliedTraversalPreference The applied route-planning preference, banned tags, and risk preference. See Request-Body Controls . riskSummary Present only when the selected route uses uncertain access. deprecations Machine-readable notices for response fields that consumers should replace. filesLoaded , cacheKey , fromCache Quick-demo service metadata. These fields do not describe the route or the TopoScript language. The structured response does not currently contain a top-level numeric total. A consumer can obtain the route's total traversal time by summing costSeconds across all steps."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Step Order and Types","href":"routing/route-results.html#step-order-and-types","text":"steps is in traversal order. Each step number is one-based and increases contiguously. type Meaning Source relationship Walk One or more consecutive walking edges on one map. Generated from traversed atomic-path declarations. WalkStraight A walking group recognized as part of one declared straight line. Generated when traversed atomic paths match a linear-path . Transport An elevator or escalator ride between maps. Generated from a selected transport ride. Climbing A stair traversal between maps. Generated from a selected stairs transport. Consecutive walking edges on the same map are grouped into one Walk step unless spatial hints split them into WalkStraight steps. Every non-walking route edge becomes its own transport or climbing step."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Walking-Step Fields","href":"routing/route-results.html#walking-step-fields","text":"Field Presence Meaning step , type Always Sequence number and Walk or WalkStraight . graph Always The identifier of the containing topo map . from , to Always Human-readable endpoint strings assembled from node identifiers and descriptions. Prefer waypoints for machine use. waypoints Always Structured, ordered node records traversed by the step. namedWaypoints Currently present Legacy human-readable strings. This field is marked for deprecation; use waypoints . costSeconds Always The sum of the traversed atomic-path costs , in seconds. tags Always The distinct, sorted tags collected from the step's atomic paths. requiredActions , requiredActionEvents Always Application-facing actions collected from the traversed paths, plus their exact edge locations. uncertainAccess Conditional Present when the step uses one or more uncertain paths. turnDirection , turnAtNode Conditional on WalkStraight A resolved turn hint for entering this straight segment."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Transport and Climbing Step Fields","href":"routing/route-results.html#transport-step-fields","text":"Field Meaning step , type The sequence number and either Transport or Climbing . description A human-readable sentence identifying the transport and endpoint maps, such as Take OfficeExpress from Lobby to OfficeLevel30 . fromGraph , toGraph The identifiers of the departure and arrival maps . waypoints The ordered departure and arrival landing nodes . costSeconds The calculated ride or climbing time in seconds. Its calculation depends on the elevator , escalator , or stair model. tags , requiredActions , requiredActionEvents Present as arrays. Current transport rides do not contribute transport-level tags or required actions. uncertainAccess Present when the selected ride uses uncertain station access. Walking to a landing, walking between two landing nodes during a transfer, and walking away from a landing are ordinary walking steps. Transport entry, ride, transfer, and exit are therefore not encoded as four artificial event types: the ordered walking and transport steps preserve the actual modeled movement."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Waypoints, Identifiers, and Descriptions","href":"routing/route-results.html#waypoints-and-identifiers","text":"Each waypoint contains nodeId and graph . Together they identify the source node as graph::nodeId , matching the qualified-name form used by route requests and transport stations . The structured waypoint projection also includes the recognized node properties : description , shop and facility names and categories, positions and throughput data, tags , narration , refVisualUrl , and beaconId . Missing optional values are serialized as null ; missing node tags are serialized as an empty array. isIntermediate states whether a waypoint lies between the first and last waypoint of that step. isTrivial identifies a node that normally needs no standalone presentation; an explicit isTrivial node property overrides the default classification. Generated internal routing nodes are omitted from the waypoint projection. Consumers should use structured waypoint fields for labels and localized narration rather than parsing from , to , path , or a transport step's description ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Cost, Duration, and Distance","href":"routing/route-results.html#cost-duration-and-distance","text":"costSeconds is a floating-point traversal-time value in seconds. The route cost is the sum of walking, transfer-walking, elevator, escalator, and stair time described by Route-Cost Composition . The current route result does not expose geometric distance. Node coordinates, estimated coordinates, and arbitrary application annotations must not be interpreted as a route-distance field."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Required Actions","href":"routing/route-results.html#required-actions","text":"requiredActions is a de-duplicated list of actions collected from the step's path annotations . requiredActionEvents preserves where each action occurs: { \"action\": \"tap_access_card\", \"from\": \"public_hall\", \"to\": \"controlled_door\", \"graph\": \"OfficeLevel30\" } Actions inform the consuming application. They do not change route eligibility, traversal cost, or ranking. The recognized source keys and their behavior are defined in Required Actions and Other Annotations ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Uncertain Access and Access Failures","href":"routing/route-results.html#uncertain-access-output","text":"A returned step that uses uncertain access contains: \"uncertainAccess\": { \"uncertaintyReasons\": [ { \"type\": \"CardAccess\", \"reason\": \"The floor button may require a card.\" } ], \"message\": \"This segment may require additional access and may not always be available.\" } The successful response also contains a top-level riskSummary with routeNotNecessarilyAvailable , the applied risk preference, aggregated uncertainty reasons, and a disclaimer. A failed hard requires condition removes the affected path, station permission, or ride from the compiled routing model. It is not emitted as a warning on an otherwise successful route. If the remaining model has no complete route, the request produces a no-route response."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Spatial and Turn Hints","href":"routing/route-results.html#spatial-and-turn-hints","text":"Placeholder: how source-level spatial hints shape structured walking steps. A linear-path can divide a walking route into WalkStraight steps. It does not create connectivity or add cost. When a matching directional-arrow exists at the boundary between straight segments, the later segment may contain: { \"type\": \"WalkStraight\", \"turnDirection\": \"LEFT\", \"turnAtNode\": \"atrium_junction\" } turnDirection is resolved for the route's actual direction of travel. turnAtNode is the arrow's anchor node, where the turn applies. When no matching hint exists, both fields are absent."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Empty, Failed, and No-Route Results","href":"routing/route-results.html#empty-and-failed-results","text":""},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Origin equals destination","href":"routing/route-results.html#empty-and-failed-results","text":"The route is successful, steps is empty, and the human-readable path states that no movement is required."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"No complete route","href":"routing/route-results.html#empty-and-failed-results","text":"The service returns an error response rather than a partial route. Common codes include NO_ROUTE_FOUND , NO_ROUTE_FOR_USER_PARAMS , and NO_ROUTE_WITH_BAN_TAGS ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Banned destination","href":"routing/route-results.html#empty-and-failed-results","text":"The service returns DESTINATION_HAS_BANNED_TAG with the qualified node identifier and conflicting tags."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Invalid project","href":"routing/route-results.html#empty-and-failed-results","text":"Compilation fails before routing. See Validation and Error Reporting . A route response is complete or absent. The current engine does not return a best-effort prefix ending at the point where navigation became impossible. See No-Route Outcomes ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Ordering and Optionality","href":"routing/route-results.html#ordering-and-optionality","text":"Steps and waypoints are ordered by traversal. Step numbers are one-based and contiguous. Path tags are distinct and sorted. Required actions are de-duplicated while preserving their first encountered order. turnDirection , turnAtNode , uncertainAccess , and top-level riskSummary are omitted when inapplicable. Equal-ranked alternative routes have no stable selection order. See Tie-Breaking and Determinism ."},{"page":"Compiled and Runtime Outputs","title":"Serialization and Compatibility","href":"routing/route-results.html#serialization-and-compatibility","text":"Structured output uses JSON objects, arrays, strings, numbers, Booleans, and null . Applications should consume documented structured fields and tolerate the addition of new optional fields. The exact text of path , from , to , transport description , disclaimers, and error messages is human-readable output rather than a stable identifier. Applications must not branch on that wording. The response's deprecations array currently marks steps[].namedWaypoints as scheduled for replacement by steps[].waypoints . Consumers should migrate to the structured field. REST fields such as HTTP status, cache state, and loaded-file count belong to the web adapter. An MCP server, editor, or agent may use a different envelope while preserving the route-step meanings defined here."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Routing Semantics","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html","text":"Routing Semantics Route planning searches a compiled building model for a complete traversable route between two nodes. The result may combine walking within topo maps, transfers between transport landings, and cross-map travel by elevators, escalators, or stairs."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Compiled Routing Model","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#compiled-routing-model","text":"Placeholder: per-map navigation graphs connected through transport stations and cross-map transport edges. The compiled building contains one directed navigation graph for each topo map and one transport graph connecting their landing nodes. Each directed atomic path becomes one walking edge. Each bidirectional atomic path becomes two directed walking edges. Each permitted transport ride becomes a directed cross-map edge. A transfer between transports exists only when the two landing nodes are connected by a traversable route on their shared map. Hard constraints and ride policies have already shaped this model before route planning begins. See Hard Access Constraints ."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Route Request","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#route-request","text":"A route request identifies a compiled building, an origin node, a destination node, a building-routing mode, and the requested traversal preferences. Risk preference, banned tags, route-planning preference, and user parameters are supplied in the request body. The current quick-demo endpoint receives the building name, origin, destination, and isHighRise selector as query parameters. POST /api/v1/quick-demo-navigation ?buildingName=SkyrimTower &startNode=Lobby::main_entrance &endNode=OfficeLevel30::reception &isHighRise=true { \"userParams\": { \"officeAccess\": true, \"timeOfDayInSeconds\": 50400 }, \"traversalPreference\": { \"routePlanningPreference\": \"MinimizeTime\", \"banTags\": [\"outdoor\"], \"riskPreference\": \"conservative\" } } For request construction and endpoint-specific behavior, see the Navigation Request Guide ."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Origin and Destination Resolution","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#endpoint-resolution","text":"A qualified node identifier has the form MapName::NodeName . Qualified identifiers are strongly recommended because node identifiers are local to their containing maps. An unqualified node identifier is accepted only when exactly one compiled map contains a node with that identifier. The request is invalid when the named map does not exist, the named node does not exist in that map, an unqualified name matches no node, or an unqualified name matches nodes in multiple maps. When the origin and destination are the same node, the result contains no movement."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Same-Map Routing","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#same-map-routing","text":"When both endpoints belong to the same topo map, the engine searches only that map. Transport services are not considered, even if leaving and re-entering the map could theoretically produce a lower cost. The selected route minimizes the sum of its atomic-path costs while respecting path direction, hard access restrictions, banned tags, and uncertain-access policy. This is an exact shortest-path search when all atomic-path costs are non-negative and the compiled topology is valid."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Fully Informed Low-Rise Routing","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#low-rise-routing","text":"Placeholder: an end-to-end search comparing complete walking, transfer, and transport costs. With isHighRise=false , cross-map routing uses a fully informed hierarchical search. Its hierarchy contains the exact origin, the exact destination, and every transport landing in the building. For each floor, the engine calculates shortest walking routes between relevant hierarchy nodes. These walking routes are combined with permitted transport rides in one building-wide cost search. The resulting total includes: walking from the origin to the first transport; cross-map transport travel; walking transfers between transport landings; additional transport travel; and walking from the final transport to the destination. A slower transport near the origin may therefore be selected instead of a faster transport that requires a long walk. Route A 100 seconds walking + 1 second express ride + 100 seconds walking Total: 201 seconds Route B 1 second walking + 10 second ride + 1 second transfer + 10 second ride + 1 second walking Total: 23 seconds Fully informed routing selects Route B under MinimizeTime . The current low-rise search always minimizes total traversal cost. MinimizeTransfers and MinimizePhysicalDemands do not change low-rise route selection."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"High-Rise Routing","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#high-rise-routing","text":"Placeholder: transport plans are ranked first, then connected and validated through floor-level walking routes. With isHighRise=true , cross-map routing uses a transport-first strategy. Find transport stations on the origin and destination maps. Calculate a transport plan for each origin-station and destination-station pairing. Rank the resulting transport plans according to the requested route preference. Connect the chosen stations using floor-level walking routes. Try the next transport plan if a required walking connection is unavailable. Origin, destination, and transfer walking costs are included in the returned route. Origin and destination walking costs do not participate in the initial transport-plan ranking. High-rise routing is therefore a transport-prioritized heuristic. It is suitable when transport selection is expected to dominate the journey, but it does not guarantee the globally lowest end-to-end cost. For each origin-station and destination-station pairing, the candidate set retains only its lowest-cost transport path. Alternative transport paths between the same station pair are not independently ranked by the other preferences. The isHighRise value selects a routing strategy. It is not checked against the building's physical height or floor count. The quick-demo endpoint defaults it to true ."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Transport Discovery and Selection","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#transport-selection","text":"Only transports selected by building-includes participate in routing. A directed ride is available only when: both maps are stations of the transport; departure is permitted at the origin station; arrival is permitted at the destination station; the applicable ride-from policy permits the ordered pair; and the request's uncertain-access policy permits the ride. An express transport serves only its declared stations. Passing the physical level of an undeclared floor does not make that floor reachable. Changing between transports requires a traversable atomic-path route between their landing nodes. Serving the same floor does not create a zero-cost transfer. See Transfers Between Transports ."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Route-Cost Composition","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#route-cost","text":"total route cost = walking-path costs + transfer-walking costs + elevator costs + escalator costs + stair costs Atomic-path cost values represent traversal time in seconds. Elevator cost includes estimated waiting and movement time, escalators use their declared fixed travel time, stairs use calculated climbing time, and transfers use the walking cost between landing nodes. Uncertain access does not add a numeric penalty. The risk preference determines whether an uncertain traversal may participate. Tags, required actions, and other application annotations do not add or subtract route cost."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Exact and Heuristic Behavior","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#exactness","text":"Situation Current behavior Same-map routing Exact minimum atomic-path cost. Low-rise MinimizeTime Exact end-to-end minimum cost across walking and transport. Low-rise other preferences The same cost-minimizing behavior as MinimizeTime . High-rise MinimizeTime Lowest-cost transport plan first; terminal walking is excluded from initial ranking. High-rise MinimizeTransfers Transport plans are ranked by transport-graph edge count, then transport cost. High-rise MinimizePhysicalDemands Transport plans are ranked by their transport physical-demand score. Equal-ranked alternatives No stable public tie-breaking order."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"Tie-Breaking and Determinism","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#ties-and-determinism","text":"A strictly cheaper candidate replaces a more expensive candidate. An equal-cost candidate does not replace the route already retained. Source declaration order, lexical identifier order, and station order are not public tie-breakers. When several routes have equal ranking values, the selected route is unspecified and may change between engine versions. A fixed request with one uniquely best route produces a stable result. Applications must not depend on the identity of one particular route among exact ties."},{"page":"Routing Semantics","title":"No-Route Outcomes","href":"routing/routing-semantics.html#no-route","text":"A route may be unavailable because: the origin or destination cannot be resolved; the directed topology does not connect them; an unsatisfied hard constraint removed a required path or ride; a transport station prohibits arrival or departure; a ride-from policy prohibits the required journey; banned tags eliminate every candidate; uncertain access is disallowed and no certain route remains; or required transport transfers are not connected within their floor maps. The public service distinguishes DESTINATION_HAS_BANNED_TAG , NO_ROUTE_WITH_BAN_TAGS , NO_ROUTE_FOR_USER_PARAMS , and NO_ROUTE_FOUND . A route request returns either a complete route or a no-route outcome. Partial routes are not returned."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Nodes","href":"topology/nodes.html","text":"Nodes A node is a named topological entity representing one unambiguous, physically bounded area of a venue. Nodes belong to one topo map and therefore to one certain floor."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Physical Semantics","href":"topology/nodes.html#physical-semantics","text":"A node may represent a destination area, entrance area, doorway area, junction area, corridor-corner area, transport-landing area, decision area, or another navigation-relevant region. The physical areas represented by any two distinct nodes must not overlap. Under Region Connection Calculus topology, their relationship must be one of: Disconnected ( DC ) : the two areas do not share any physical point. Externally connected ( EC ) : the boundaries of the two areas touch, but their interiors do not intersect. Distinct nodes must therefore not partially overlap, represent equal physical areas, or contain one another. If a physical region would otherwise be represented by overlapping nodes, the author must partition it into non-overlapping node areas."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Recommended Granularity","href":"topology/nodes.html#recommended-granularity","text":"As a mapping guideline, a node should be small enough that the longest reasonable walking line across its area takes no more than five seconds. This is the node's longest “diagonal” in walking-time terms; it does not require metric coordinates or a geometrically rectangular area. If crossing the represented area can take longer than five seconds, consider partitioning it into finer, non-overlapping nodes connected by atomic paths. This threshold is an authoring recommendation and is not enforced by the compiler."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Syntax","href":"topology/nodes.html#syntax","text":"topo-node east_lift_lobby A node may carry an annotation record: topo-node visitor_reception { description = \"Visitor reception desk\", facilityName = \"Reception\", tags = [\"staffed\"], narration = \"explicit\" } The node identifier is local to its containing topo map. Local paths and navigation-hint declarations use the simple name. A transport refers to the node with a qualified name such as MallLevel5::east_lift_lobby . Node identifiers must be unique within each topo map. A repeated identifier is rejected during parsing, even when both declarations have identical annotations; the error names the node and its containing map. Identifiers are case-sensitive, and separate maps or instances of a reusable map may use the same local node names."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Supported Properties","href":"topology/nodes.html#properties","text":"The annotation record is optional. Integer, floating-point, Boolean, string, and list values are preserved as compiled node attributes and may be exposed to applications. The TopoNavi Engine's structured route output recognizes and exposes these node fields when present: description shopName or shop_name facilityName or facility_name shopCategory or shop_category facilityCategory or facility_category positions observedThroughput or observed_throughput maxThroughput or max_throughput tags narration refVisualUrl or ref_visual_url beaconId or beacon_id isTrivial or is_trivial Other attributes are application-defined. They are retained in the compiled node data, but TopoScript does not prescribe how every application must interpret them."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Tags","href":"topology/nodes.html#node-tags","text":"Store node tags as a list of canonical strings in tags . For placement rules and the supported vocabulary, read the TopoScript Tagging Guide ."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Connectivity","href":"topology/nodes.html#connectivity","text":"A node may participate in zero or more atomic paths and may be referenced by one or more transport stations. Declaring a node alone does not make it reachable."},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Valid Partition Example","href":"topology/nodes.html#valid-partition","text":"A long concourse containing a junction and two lift lobbies should be partitioned into distinct regions: topo-node west_lift_lobby topo-node central_junction topo-node east_lift_lobby atomic-path [west_lift_lobby central_junction] { cost = 18.0 } atomic-path [central_junction east_lift_lobby] { cost = 16.0 }"},{"page":"Nodes","title":"Invalid Overlap Example","href":"topology/nodes.html#invalid-overlap","text":"Do not model the same physical junction once as part of a corridor node and again as a separate junction node. The two represented areas would overlap. Partition the corridor at the junction and connect the resulting nodes instead."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Paths and Local Movement","href":"topology/paths.html","text":"Paths and Local Movement An atomic path declares direct, traversable connectivity between two nodes in the same topo map. Atomic paths are the weighted edges used for intra-floor route calculation."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Atomicity","href":"topology/paths.html#atomicity","text":"An atomic path must connect exactly two nodes without physically crossing another intermediate node. If traveling from one represented place to another necessarily makes a person pass through a third node, the connection must be split. For nodes A , B , and C encountered in that order, declare: atomic-path [A B] { cost = 8.0 } atomic-path [B C] { cost = 6.0 } Do not also declare A C when every physical traversal from A to C necessarily passes through B . Such an edge would hide a real decision or passage point from the topology."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Syntax and Direction","href":"topology/paths.html#syntax-and-direction","text":"A directed path permits traversal only from the left node to the right node: atomic-path [entry_gate -> paid_concourse] { cost = 4.0 } A bidirectional path creates traversal in both directions with the same cost and attributes: atomic-path [north_corridor central_junction] { cost = 9.0 } If the directions have different costs or annotations, declare two directed atomic paths: atomic-path [lower_ramp -> upper_ramp] { cost = 15.0, tags = [\"up_slope\"] } atomic-path [upper_ramp -> lower_ramp] { cost = 10.0, tags = [\"down_slope\"] }"},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Path Cost","href":"topology/paths.html#cost","text":"Every atomic path must contain a numeric cost field: atomic-path [atrium -> information_desk] { cost = 7.5 } The value may be an Int or Float and must be non-negative. The routing algorithm combines path costs to compare candidate routes. For each path included after access-constraint evaluation, the compiler checks the evaluated cost before constructing graph edges. Negative literals and expressions that evaluate to negative values fail compilation with the map and endpoint names in the error. Zero-cost paths are permitted, and the same check applies to directed and bidirectional paths. cost is the only atomic-path weight required by the TopoNavi Engine. TopoScript does not require separate distance or time fields. Applications may add such annotations, but their meaning and use are application-defined."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Path Properties","href":"topology/paths.html#path-properties","text":"Fields other than cost are annotations. Primitive values and lists are preserved in the compiled path and may be exposed verbatim through application responses. atomic-path [shopfront -> outdoor_terrace] { cost = 5.0, tags = [\"door\", \"outdoor\", \"rain_exposed\"], doorLabel = \"Terrace Door T5\" } The TopoNavi Engine recognizes tags for traversal filtering and route metadata. The meaning of custom fields such as doorLabel belongs to the consuming application."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Tags","href":"topology/paths.html#path-tags","text":"Store path tags as a list of canonical strings in tags . For placement rules and the supported vocabulary, read the TopoScript Tagging Guide ."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Uncertain Access","href":"topology/paths.html#uncertain-access","text":"subject-to records that a path remains physically present but may be unavailable: atomic-path [public_gallery roof_terrace] { cost = 8.0 } subject-to InGoodWeather because \"The terrace may close in severe weather.\" The path remains in the compiled topology. Full syntax and examples are provided in Representing Uncertain Access . Routing-time selection, propagation, and disclosure belong to Uncertain Access and Risk Handling ."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Conditional Topology and Constraint Attachment Points","href":"topology/paths.html#conditional-topology","text":"Placeholder: this section is deferred until the intended scope of hard, compile-time topology conditions and their permitted attachment points is confirmed."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Straight-Line Hints","href":"topology/paths.html#linear-paths","text":"A linear-path lists at least three local nodes in their physical order along one straight corridor or other straight walking line: linear-path [west_lift_lobby, atrium_junction, east_lift_lobby] This declaration does not create connectivity. The required atomic paths must still be declared separately. The hint allows route output to group matching walking edges into straight-line legs."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Turn Hints","href":"topology/paths.html#directional-arrows","text":"A directional-arrow states where a target node lies relative to a facing direction established at an anchor node: directional-arrow [ANCHOR -> REFERENCE] TARGET DIRECTION"},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"ANCHOR","href":"topology/paths.html#directional-arrows","text":"The node where the turn or directional instruction applies."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"REFERENCE","href":"topology/paths.html#directional-arrows","text":"The node used to establish which way the traveler is facing while at the anchor."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"TARGET","href":"topology/paths.html#directional-arrows","text":"The node whose position is being described relative to that facing direction. directional-arrow [atrium_junction -> west_lift_lobby] food_court_entrance LEFT In this example, the anchor is atrium_junction . While standing there and facing toward the reference node west_lift_lobby , the target food_court_entrance is on the traveler's left. With -> , a traveler at the anchor faces toward the reference node. With , the traveler at the anchor faces away from the reference node: directional-arrow [atrium_junction The supported relative directions are FRONT , FRONT_RIGHT , RIGHT , REAR_RIGHT , REAR , REAR_LEFT , LEFT , and FRONT_LEFT . A directional arrow neither creates a path nor changes route cost. When it matches a boundary between straight-line legs, structured route output may expose the resolved turn direction and the node at which the turn occurs."},{"page":"Paths and Local Movement","title":"Validation","href":"topology/paths.html#path-validation","text":"Both endpoints must resolve to nodes in the containing topo map. cost must be an Int or Float and must not be negative. A linear-path must contain at least three declared nodes. Every node named by a directional arrow must exist in the containing topo map. A direction name in a directional arrow must be one of the eight supported values."},{"page":"Map Selection and Reuse","title":"Map Selection and Reuse","href":"topology/submaps.html","text":"Map Selection and Reuse A topo-map source defines local floor topology. It becomes part of a building only when selected as a submap by building-includes . A reusable source may produce multiple named instances, but each instance represents one particular floor."},{"page":"Map Selection and Reuse","title":"Where Submaps Are Declared","href":"topology/submaps.html#where-submaps-are-declared","text":"submap is a whole-building composition entry, not a declaration nested inside a topo map. Direct selection, source resolution, reusable using instances, ordering, and management-domain attachment are documented in Whole-Building Composition ."},{"page":"Map Selection and Reuse","title":"No Parent-Map Hierarchy","href":"topology/submaps.html#no-parent-map","text":"Current TopoScript does not define parent and child topo maps. Included map instances are sibling navigation graphs in the compiled building. The root source is a shared declaration environment, not a parent map. building-includes is the project manifest, not a navigable map. Neither contains the physical topology of its included floors."},{"page":"Map Selection and Reuse","title":"Cross-Map Connections","href":"topology/submaps.html#cross-map-connections","text":"An atomic path cannot connect nodes from different topo maps. Cross-map movement is declared by a transport whose stations refer to nodes in the participating floor maps."},{"page":"Map Selection and Reuse","title":"Instantiated-Content Names","href":"topology/submaps.html#deferred-instance-qualification","text":"Deferred: the precise reference rules and collision behavior for content instantiated through submap InstanceName using BaseMapName will be documented after the engine contract is confirmed."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Topo Maps","href":"topology/topomap.html","text":"Topo Maps A topo-map instance describes the local navigation topology of one certain floor. It contains the floor's nodes , atomic paths , and optional declarations used to explain local movement. A topo map describes connectivity rather than a metric floor plan. Movement between topo maps belongs to a transport declaration, not to an atomic path."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Responsibilities and Boundaries","href":"topology/topomap.html#responsibilities-and-boundaries","text":"One topo-map instance must model one certain floor. A topo map must not combine nodes from different floors merely because those nodes can be connected in one graph. A reusable topo-map source may define a standard-floor template, but every resulting instance still represents one particular floor. It is algorithmically possible to place every node of a building into one super-map, but doing so is extremely discouraged. A super-map: erases the semantic boundary between movement on a floor and movement between floors; bypasses the transport model used for elevators, escalators, and stairs; makes floor-specific management domains, access rules, route explanations, and updates harder to express and audit; prevents effective reuse of standard-floor topology; and increases the impact of a local mapping mistake by placing unrelated floors in the same graph. These are modeling and maintainability boundaries, not graph-theory limitations."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Declaration","href":"topology/topomap.html#declaration","text":"topo-map MallLevel5() { let params = {} topo-node atrium topo-node east_lift_lobby atomic-path [atrium east_lift_lobby] { cost = 12.0 } } The map name is an identifier. For a directly included map, the name in the declaration, the source filename, and the submap name in building-includes should agree exactly, including case. The header may syntactically contain a parameter list, but map-instance argument passing is not currently defined. Authors should use an empty header parameter list and place local calculated values in the map body."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"The params Record","href":"topology/topomap.html#map-parameters","text":"Every compiled topo map must define a local params record. Use an empty record when the map has no recognized parameters: let params = {} The optional recognized field permResident is an Int describing the floor's permanent resident population: let params: {permResident: Int} = { permResident = 180 } When elevator-demand modeling needs this value and it is omitted, the TopoNavi Engine uses 1 . Additional fields may be used by local expressions, but the engine does not assign them standard map-level routing semantics."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Management-Domain Override","href":"topology/topomap.html#management-domain-override","text":"A topo map may override the management domain assigned by building-includes : topo-map TradingFloor() override-managed-by TradingDomain { let params = {} } The effective-domain precedence is documented in Management-Domain Attachment ."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Intra-Map Movement","href":"topology/topomap.html#intra-map-movement","text":"Nodes and atomic paths describe movement within the map. An atomic path must therefore reference two nodes declared in the same topo map. An elevator landing, stair landing, or escalator landing is still an ordinary node in its floor map. A transport station later refers to that node and provides the cross-map connection."},{"page":"Topo Maps","title":"Disconnected and Invalid Topology","href":"topology/topomap.html#disconnected-and-invalid-topology","text":"A disconnected node or disconnected component is permitted, but no intra-map route can cross the gap. This can intentionally represent an unmapped or currently unreachable area; otherwise it usually indicates a missing atomic path. Node identifiers must be unique within a topo map. Duplicate declarations make references ambiguous and are invalid authoring practice. A path that names an undeclared node fails during elaboration. Negative path costs are invalid because routing requires non-negative weights."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Elevators","href":"transport/elevators.html","text":"Elevators An Elevator transport represents one elevator bank serving two or more topo-map instances. Its parameters describe the bank, while each station identifies a served landing and its vertical location. Common station, access, ride-policy, transfer, and output rules are defined in Cross-Map Transportation ."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Declaration","href":"transport/elevators.html#declaration","text":"transport MainElevator is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 3.0, acceleration = 0.8, duty = 1600, capacity = 18, carAmount = 4 } let displayName = \"Main passenger elevators\" station Lobby at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.40 } station Office2 at OfficeLevel2::main_elevator_landing { location = 4.2, departureRate = 0.10 } }"},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Elevator Parameters","href":"transport/elevators.html#parameters","text":"An elevator must define a local params record. The recognized fields are: Field Type Default Meaning maxVelocity Int or Float 2.5 Maximum car velocity in metres per second. acceleration Int or Float 0.8 Acceleration in metres per second squared. carAmount Int 1 Number of cars in the elevator bank. capacity Int 13 Modeled passenger capacity of each car. duty Int 1000 Rated carrying load of the elevator. maxSpeed and cars are not aliases for maxVelocity and carAmount ."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Duty and Carrying Load","href":"transport/elevators.html#duty","text":"duty is currently retained in the compiled elevator model for application-side inspection but does not affect route eligibility or route cost. It is intended to participate in a native implicit constraint in a future language version, allowing the engine to compare an elevator's duty with the requested carrying load."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Elevator Station Fields","href":"transport/elevators.html#station-fields","text":"Every elevator station requires: Field Type Meaning location Int or Float Vertical elevation in metres relative to a consistent building datum. departureRate Float Probability-like departure share used by elevator waiting-time estimation. An integer departureRate , such as departureRate = 1 , is not accepted; write 1.0 . It is strongly recommended that every departureRate remain within the inclusive range 0.0 to 1.0 , and that the values across one elevator bank total approximately 1.0 . These invariants are not currently enforced by the compiler."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Served Floors","href":"transport/elevators.html#served-floors","text":"Every declared station is a stop. Every permitted ordered pair of distinct stops is connected directly in the compiled transport graph. Stations need not be written in height order because location establishes their vertical ordering. An express elevator is modeled by omitting floors at which it does not stop: station Lobby at Lobby::express_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.50 } station SkyLobby at SkyLobby30::express_landing { location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.50 }"},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Elevator Cost Model","href":"transport/elevators.html#cost-model","text":"Movement time is estimated from the vertical distance between station location values, maxVelocity , and acceleration . Waiting-time estimation additionally uses carAmount , capacity , station departureRate values, and each served map's permResident . The current model uses an internal fifteen-second dwell-time assumption. The compiled transport graph currently calculates elevator ride costs using the TopoNavi Engine's up-rush traffic model. Traffic-pattern selection and exact route-cost behavior are implementation-defined routing concerns and are documented in the routing reference. The station with the lowest location is treated as the entrance station for waiting-time estimation; all other stations are treated as occupant stations."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Station and Ride Access","href":"transport/elevators.html#access-example","text":"station F50 at OfficeLevel50::passenger_landing { location = 222.0, departureRate = 0.04 } requires TradingFloorAccess on Arrive ride-from any to HotelDomain requires HotelAccess The station rule controls arrival at this particular stop. The ride policy controls matching directed rides across the management domain. See Station Access and Ride Policies ."},{"page":"Elevators","title":"Invalid Elevator Declarations","href":"transport/elevators.html#invalid-elevators","text":"The transport must contain a record-valued params binding. Recognized numeric parameters must have the types shown above. Every station must provide a numeric location . Every station must provide a floating-point departureRate . Every qualified station node must resolve to an included topo map and a declared node. An elevator intended for cross-map navigation must serve at least two maps."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Escalators","href":"transport/escalators.html","text":"Escalators An Escalator transport is a fixed-time connection between exactly two topo-map instances. Common station, access, ride-policy, transfer, and output rules are defined in Cross-Map Transportation ."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Declaration","href":"transport/escalators.html#declaration","text":"transport AtriumEscalator is Escalator { let params = { time = 18.0 } let displayName = \"Atrium escalator\" station Lower at MallLevel2::atrium_escalator_landing {} station Upper at MallLevel3::atrium_escalator_landing {} }"},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Fixed Travel Time","href":"transport/escalators.html#travel-time","text":"params.time is the complete travel time in seconds between the two stations. time is required. It may be an Int or Float . It must be finite and greater than zero. No additional waiting, velocity, acceleration, or dwell-time calculation is applied."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Stations","href":"transport/escalators.html#stations","text":"An escalator must declare exactly two stations, and the stations must belong to two distinct topo-map instances. Station records may be empty because escalators do not require location , departureRate , or directSegmentIndex . The declaration order is not a physical elevation or direction declaration."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Operating Direction","href":"transport/escalators.html#direction","text":"An escalator is currently modeled as a fixed-time connection that can be traversed in either direction. This model is suitable for a bidirectional escalator service, such as a paired up-and-down installation represented as one transport. A map author cannot currently declare that a particular escalator operates only upward, only downward, or reverses direction according to an operating schedule. Station access requirements and out-of-order describe authorization or availability. They do not describe the escalator's ordinary operating direction."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Station Availability","href":"transport/escalators.html#station-availability","text":"Escalators support unscoped, arrival-only, and departure-only station requirements, uncertain access, and out-of-order in the same way as elevators: station Upper at MallLevel3::atrium_escalator_landing {} requires MallOpen on Arrive See Station Access ."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Route Output","href":"transport/escalators.html#route-output","text":"An escalator ride is returned as a Transport segment with the configured fixed travel time."},{"page":"Escalators","title":"Invalid Escalator Declarations","href":"transport/escalators.html#invalid-escalators","text":"A missing or non-numeric params.time fails compilation. A non-finite, zero, or negative time fails compilation. Fewer or more than two stations fail compilation. Two stations belonging to the same topo-map instance fail compilation. An unknown station map or node fails compilation."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Stairs","href":"transport/stairs.html","text":"Stairs A Stairs transport represents a continuous staircase connecting landing nodes on two or more topo-map instances. Common station, ride-policy, transfer, and output rules are defined in Cross-Map Transportation ."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Declaration","href":"transport/stairs.html#declaration","text":"transport WestStair is Stairs { let params = { turnBackCost = 3 } let displayName = \"West staircase\" station Ground at Lobby::west_stair_landing { location = 0.0, directSegmentIndex = 0 } station Level2 at OfficeLevel2::west_stair_landing { location = 4.2, directSegmentIndex = 1 } station Level3 at OfficeLevel3::west_stair_landing { location = 8.4, directSegmentIndex = 2 } }"},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Stair Parameters","href":"transport/stairs.html#parameters","text":"A staircase must define a local params record. turnBackCost is an optional Int and defaults to 3 . turnBackCost represents additional traversal time, in seconds, for a change between direct stair segments. It is a time cost rather than a stamina or physical-demand value."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Stair Station Fields","href":"transport/stairs.html#station-fields","text":"Every stair station requires: Field Type Meaning location Int or Float Vertical elevation in metres relative to a consistent building datum. directSegmentIndex Int The ordered direct stair segment containing this landing. Indices normally begin at 0 at one end of the staircase and increase as the staircase progresses. Crossing non-adjacent segment indices incurs turnBackCost for the intermediate changes of direction."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Traversal Time","href":"transport/stairs.html#traversal-time","text":"Base stair traversal time is estimated from the absolute vertical difference between station location values using an average vertical walking speed of 0.167 metres per second. Applicable turnBackCost values represent additional traversal time. Stair traversal is returned as Climbing . Physical-demand routing may therefore penalize stair traversal more heavily than elevator or escalator use without interpreting turnBackCost as a stamina score."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Service Range","href":"transport/stairs.html#service-range","text":"Every declared landing is part of the same continuous staircase. The compiled transport connects every permitted ordered pair of distinct station maps; station declarations do not need to be limited to adjacent floors."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Stair Access Control","href":"transport/stairs.html#stair-access-control","text":"Stair stations deliberately ignore station-level requires and out-of-order . A person's ability to step onto a stair landing is determined by the surrounding floor topology rather than by the staircase transport itself. When a staircase is protected by a locked door, staff-only entrance, emergency gate, or similar boundary, attach requires to the atomic path that crosses that boundary: atomic-path [public_corridor -> protected_stair_landing] { cost = 4.0 } requires StaffAccess This controls the actual doorway or boundary while leaving the physical staircase represented. Where access differs by floor or travel direction, place the requirement on each applicable directed doorway path."},{"page":"Stairs","title":"Invalid Stair Declarations","href":"transport/stairs.html#invalid-stairs","text":"The transport must contain a record-valued params binding. turnBackCost , when present, must be an Int . Every station must provide a numeric location . Every station must provide an integer directSegmentIndex . Every qualified station node must resolve to an included topo map and a declared node. A staircase intended for cross-map navigation must serve at least two maps."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Cross-Map Transportation","href":"transport/transport.html","text":"Cross-Map Transportation A transport declares traversable connections between topo maps. Because each topo-map instance represents one floor, a transport normally describes movement between floors. A transport does not replace the landing areas inside floor maps. Each participating map declares its own landing node; a transport station references that node and connects it to the transport network."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Topology Boundary","href":"transport/transport.html#topology-boundary","text":"Atomic paths describe movement within one topo map. Transports describe movement between topo maps. An elevator hall, stair landing, or escalator landing remains an ordinary node in its floor map. Walking to the landing is described by that map's atomic paths. The transport begins and ends at the referenced landing nodes."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Project Inclusion","href":"transport/transport.html#project-inclusion","text":"A transport participates in a building only when selected by building-includes : building-includes { submap Lobby submap OfficeLevel2 vehicle MainElevator } vehicle MainElevator resolves MainElevator.ttr , falling back to the extensionless source MainElevator ."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Supported Transport Types","href":"transport/transport.html#supported-types","text":"transport MainElevator is Elevator { } transport AtriumEscalator is Escalator { } transport WestStair is Stairs { } The supported type names are exactly Elevator , Escalator , and Stairs . They are case-sensitive. Generic, user-defined, and additional transport types are not currently supported."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Common Declaration Model","href":"transport/transport.html#common-declaration-model","text":"A transport body may contain: a transport-specific local params record; local let , def , and constraint declarations; an optional string-valued displayName binding; station declarations; station access annotations; and ride-from policies. transport MainElevator is Elevator { let params = { maxVelocity = 2.5, acceleration = 0.8, carAmount = 3 } let displayName = \"Main passenger elevators\" station Lobby at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.40 } station Office2 at OfficeLevel2::main_elevator_landing { location = 4.2, departureRate = 0.10 } }"},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Stations and Landing Nodes","href":"transport/transport.html#stations","text":"station Office2 at OfficeLevel2::main_elevator_landing { location = 4.2, departureRate = 0.10 }"},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Office2","href":"transport/transport.html#stations","text":"A station label local to this transport."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"OfficeLevel2","href":"transport/transport.html#stations","text":"The topo-map instance containing the landing."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"main_elevator_landing","href":"transport/transport.html#stations","text":"The existing node used as the transport endpoint on that map. A station does not create its referenced map or node. The map must be included in the building and the node must exist in that map. Station labels should be unique within a transport. They are used by station-specific ride-from policies and are distinct from map identifiers."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Service Range and Direction","href":"transport/transport.html#service-range","text":"The declared stations define the maps served by a transport. An unlisted map is not a stop. For elevators and stairs, the engine connects every permitted ordered pair of distinct station maps. Declaration order does not limit travel to adjacent stations. An express service is modeled by declaring only the maps at which it stops. An escalator has a specialized two-station service model described in Escalators ."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Station Access","href":"transport/transport.html#station-access","text":"A hard station requirement controls whether an elevator or escalator station may be used for arrival, departure, or both: station TradingFloor at OfficeLevel50::passenger_landing { location = 222.0, departureRate = 0.04 } requires TradingFloorAccess on Arrive Form Effect when the constraint is unsatisfied requires Access Neither arrival nor departure is permitted. requires Access on Arrive Arrival is prohibited; departure remains permitted. requires Access on Depart Departure is prohibited; arrival remains permitted. A station may be completely disabled: station F18 at OfficeLevel18::passenger_landing { location = 84.0, departureRate = 0.04 } out-of-order out-of-order prohibits both arrival and departure for elevators and escalators. Stair access is modeled differently; see Stair Access Control . Constraints use root or transport-local declarations and are evaluated during compilation. Changing a supplied parameter requires a separately compiled building model. See Compilation Time and Routing Time ."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Uncertain Station Access","href":"transport/transport.html#uncertain-station-access","text":"subject-to records uncertain station availability without removing the transport from the compiled model: station Roof at RoofLevel::lift_landing { location = 180.0, departureRate = 0.05 } subject-to RoofLiftAvailable because \"The roof lift may close in severe weather.\" on Arrive Routing-time selection, propagation, and disclosure are documented in Uncertain Access and Risk Handling ."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Ride Policies","href":"transport/transport.html#ride-policies","text":"A ride-from declaration controls matching directed rides according to one or more named constraints: ride-from any to HotelDomain requires HotelAccess ride-from ServiceLobby to OfficeDomain requires Each operand must be any , a station label declared by this transport, or an effective management-domain name. A topo-map identifier is not a ride-policy operand merely because a station serves that map. An unsatisfied rule removes matching rides in the declared direction. It does not affect the reverse direction. A satisfied rule permits its matching pairs, subject to station permissions and other applicable rules. A pair not matched by any rule remains permitted; ride-from declarations are not a whitelist. If several rules match, the rule with the highest specificity applies: station to station; station to domain; domain to station; domain to domain; any to station; station to any ; any to domain; domain to any ; any to any . A station label takes precedence over a same-named management domain. Conflicting rules for the same operands and specificity are invalid. Management-domain attachment and override precedence are defined in Management-Domain Attachment ."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Transfers Between Transports","href":"transport/transport.html#transfers","text":"When two transports serve the same topo map, transfer between them requires an intra-map walking route between their landing nodes. /* Inside OfficeExpress */ station F30 at SkyLobby30::express_landing { location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.40 } /* Inside OfficeLocal */ station F30 at SkyLobby30::local_landing { location = 138.0, departureRate = 0.10 } The transfer cost is the atomic-path cost of walking from express_landing to local_landing . If no permitted path connects the landing nodes, the transfer is unavailable on that map."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Entry, Exit, Transfer, and Ride Costs","href":"transport/transport.html#cost-composition","text":"Walking to or from a landing is represented by the floor map's atomic paths. TopoScript does not declare separate transport entry or exit costs. Elevator cost combines estimated waiting time and movement time. Escalator cost is the declared fixed travel time. Stair cost represents climbing time. A same-floor transfer uses the walking cost between landing nodes. End-to-end route-cost composition and routing preferences belong to the routing reference."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Route Output","href":"transport/transport.html#route-output","text":"Elevator and escalator rides are returned as Transport segments. Stair traversal is returned as Climbing . Current movement descriptions identify the transport and endpoint maps: Take MainElevator from Lobby to OfficeLevel2 displayName and station labels are retained in the compiled transport model, while route-step descriptions currently use the transport identifier. Transport-level tags, arbitrary annotation records, required actions, and navigation hints are not supported."},{"page":"Cross-Map Transportation","title":"Invalid References and Declarations","href":"transport/transport.html#invalid-references","text":"An unsupported transport type fails parsing or transport elaboration. A station referencing an unknown map or node fails compilation. An unknown station label or management domain in ride-from fails compilation. Duplicate station labels make station-specific policies ambiguous and are invalid. A transport with fewer than two usable station maps cannot provide cross-map movement. Transport-specific missing or incorrectly typed fields fail compilation as described in the specialized articles."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Validation and Error Reporting","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html","text":"Validation and Error Reporting A TopoScript project is valid when its selected sources can be parsed, elaborated, evaluated, and linked into a compiled building model. Successful compilation establishes structural language validity. It does not guarantee that every real-world route is available, that application-defined annotations are accurate, or that the modeled venue remains unchanged."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Validation Scope","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#validation-scope","text":"Project validation covers: the whole-building configuration and selected project sources; lexical and grammatical correctness; name resolution, scopes, types, and expression evaluation; topo-map nodes and paths; transport declarations, stations, and ride policies; and cross-file references required to construct the navigation and transport graphs. Route availability for a particular origin, destination, access context, or routing preference is a routing result rather than a project-validity test. See No-Route Outcomes ."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Validation Phases","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#validation-phases","text":"Placeholder: validation from project sources to the compiled building model. Project loading and composition: resolve the configuration, root source, selected topo maps, reusable map bases, and transports. Parsing: recognize TopoScript tokens, expressions, declarations, and source-specific top-level forms. Elaboration and evaluation: resolve visible names, check expression types, evaluate parameters and constraints, and construct map and transport declarations. Graph construction and linking: build per-map navigation graphs, resolve qualified station endpoints, validate transport-specific invariants, and construct the cross-map transport graph. The distinction between source declarations, compilation, and route planning is defined in Language Lifecycle ."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Common Invalidity Classes","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#invalidity-classes","text":"Class Examples Governing reference Syntax Invalid tokens, malformed declarations, incomplete expressions, or the wrong top-level source form. Lexical Structure and the syntax section for the relevant declaration. Names and types Unknown symbols, invisible local names, incompatible expression types, missing record fields, or a non-Boolean constraint. Identifiers and Qualified Names , Name Scopes and Visibility , and Types . Project composition Missing selected sources, unresolved reusable bases, invalid cross-file references, or mismatched project names. Whole-Building Composition . Topology Unknown path endpoints, missing path cost, invalid direction declarations, or malformed topo-map data. Topo Maps , Nodes , and Paths and Local Movement . Transport Unknown landing nodes, invalid station fields, unsupported transport types, invalid service ranges, or conflicting ride policies. Cross-Map Transportation and the type-specific elevator, escalator, or stair article."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Current Reporting Limits","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#reporting-limits","text":"Parsing failures may identify and highlight a source position. Errors discovered during later compilation phases may provide only a human-readable description without an attached source range. Validation may stop after reporting one failure. A single run is not guaranteed to return every independent problem in the project. Human-readable error text is intended to help authors correct their source, but its exact wording is not a stable API. Applications and editor integrations must not use message text as a persistent diagnostic identifier."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Correcting an Invalid Project","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#correcting-invalid-projects","text":""},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Missing Path Cost","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#correcting-invalid-projects","text":"Every atomic path requires a numeric, non-negative cost . Invalid: atomic-path [lobby -> reception] { tags = [\"indoor\"] } Corrected: atomic-path [lobby -> reception] { cost = 6, tags = [\"indoor\"] } See Path Cost ."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Unknown Transport Landing","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#correcting-invalid-projects","text":"A station must reference an existing node in a compiled map. Invalid when Lobby does not declare main_lift : station LobbyStop at Lobby::main_lift { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.25 } Corrected using the landing node declared by the map: station LobbyStop at Lobby::main_elevator_landing { location = 0.0, departureRate = 0.25 } See Stations and Landing Nodes ."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Non-Boolean Constraint","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#correcting-invalid-projects","text":"The expression required by a constraint must evaluate to Bool . Invalid: root Building(officeAccess: Int) { constraint OfficeAccess { require officeAccess } } Corrected: root Building(officeAccess: Bool) { constraint OfficeAccess { require officeAccess } } See Global Constraints ."},{"page":"Validation and Error Reporting","title":"Invalid Escalator Service Range","href":"validation/validation-and-error-reporting.html#correcting-invalid-projects","text":"An escalator must contain exactly two stations belonging to two distinct maps. Invalid: transport AtriumEscalator is Escalator { let params = { time = 18.0 } station Lower at Mall2::escalator_landing {} station Middle at Mall3::escalator_landing {} station Upper at Mall4::escalator_landing {} } Corrected: transport AtriumEscalator is Escalator { let params = { time = 18.0 } station Lower at Mall2::escalator_landing {} station Upper at Mall3::escalator_landing {} } See Escalator Stations ."}];
